#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Warn an agent session before the kernel OOM-kills it, while it can still shed work.

Why this exists: on 2026-10-27T05:55:14Z the kernel OOM-killed the Verified Linux owner
session (pid 3398496, `omp`, anon-rss 15522968 kB) inside its own systemd scope,
/user.slice/user-1000.slice/user@1000.service/app.slice/run-u174595.scope, whose MemoryMax
is 24 GiB (`memory: usage 25165824kB, limit 25165824kB, failcnt 121421`). About 40 in-session
subagent lanes died with it and nothing warned first. (dispatch://INFRA-602)

What this watch is NOT: it kills nothing and sheds nothing. Prevention belongs to the session --
a session's own self-watch (for example /mnt/nvme/sami/tmp/verified-linux/handover/ol-rss-watch.sh,
which warns at 10 GiB of omp RSS) warns first and the session sheds its own lanes. This watch
detects, and alerts the session itself and its stream lead.

It acts in three bounded ways (Sami, 2026-11-02 01:00Z: kill the runaway subprocess instead of the
whole session, and restart the sessions that did die):

  * oom bias (`oom_bias_pass`, off with --no-oom-bias): every process in a capped omp scope except
    the session's own omp is raised to oom_score_adj +500, so when the scope reaches its ceiling
    the kernel kills the runaway child, not the session;
  * pane restart (`restart_pass`, off with --no-restart, a dry run with --restart-dry-run): a
    tmux pane whose omp died without a normal `session_exit` is resumed in place;
  * scopes (`scope_adopt_pass` and `broker_scope_pass`, off with --no-adopt-scopes): a session
    with no capped OOMPolicy=continue scope gets one, and each omp worker broker and each lane's
    broker services leave the session scope they inherited for scopes of their own.

What the alerting part of one run does, with /proc and /sys reads only, no transcript reading
and no model call:

  1. Find every `omp` process on this machine and the systemd scope each one runs in
     (/proc/<pid>/cgroup). `shims/omp` starts each session under
     `systemd-run --user --scope -p MemoryMax=$OMP_MEMORY_MAX -p MemorySwapMax=0`, so the
     scope -- not the process -- is what the kernel's memcg OOM killer measures.
  2. For each such scope, read the limit it is heading for: the smallest numeric `memory.max`
     from the scope up to the cgroup root. When every level says `max`, the ceiling is the
     machine's RAM and a crossing there is a box-wide OOM, which is said in the alert.
  3. Read what the kernel cannot reclaim: `anon + shmem + kernel + sock` from the scope's
     memory.stat. A scope `shims/omp` starts carries MemorySwapMax=0, so its anonymous memory
     cannot be paged out even though the box now has a 64 GiB swap file: file cache is the only
     reclaimable part of its memory.current and must not count towards the ceiling. (A scope
     that `ossv-worker` starts sets MemoryMax alone and leaves memory.swap.max at `max`, so its
     anonymous memory CAN be paged out; the watch reads memory.swap.max per scope and says which
     shape it is, rather than claiming MemorySwapMax=0 for every scope.) This is a better
     predictor than the omp process's RSS: at the 05:55:14Z kill the omp process held
     14.9 GiB of the 24 GiB that hit the ceiling and 20 python3 tool subprocesses in the same
     scope held the rest, so an RSS threshold alone would have been watching 62% of the number
     the kernel was about to compare.
  4. A scope is CROSSING while that number is at or above `--fraction` of its limit. It must
     stay so for the grace window (default 60 s) before it alerts, so one transient compile
     does not fire. It clears below `--clear-fraction` of the limit, or when the process ends.
  5. Separately, and with no hold at all, a scope that REACHES its wall is reported: the `max`
     counter of its memory.events rises (the kernel refused an allocation at memory.max and had
     to reclaim), or memory.peak crosses `--wall-fraction` of the ceiling. Steps 3 and 4 miss
     this whenever the pressure is reclaimable: on ossv-agent-open on 2026-10-30, 31 of 95
     worker scopes had touched their 24 GiB wall while every one of them sat under the 45%
     sampling level on unreclaimable memory, and only 4 were ever reported. A touch is rate
     limited to one report per scope per `--wall-cooldown` (default 1 h) and is ONE message,
     to the session owning the scope. A touch with oom and oom_kill both 0 and at least
     `--wall-quiet-reclaimable` (0.50) of the ceiling in reclaimable page cache is not told at
     all: that is a capped job working as designed (jailed `omp -p` readers on an 8 GiB scope
     with 76-94% of it page cache, 2026-11-04). It is counted, and the next real report says so.

Alerts go over Envoy to agent sessions only, never to a person, and each is at most 600
characters ending in `Full report: <path>` (the whole detail, under reports/ beside the state
file, kept 7 days). The session itself hears its crossing, its clear and its wall touches: it
owns the shedding. The box-health owner -- the holder of the `lead-box-health` Envoy role, and
the SRE session when nobody holds it -- hears only what the session cannot settle: a crossing
still rising 10 minutes after its alert, an OOM kill during a crossing, a crossing or wall
touch whose session cannot be named, and 3 or more wall touches on the box inside 15 minutes.
It never hears a clear.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import calendar
import fcntl
import hashlib
import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import shutil
import socket
import subprocess
import sys
import time
import urllib.error
import urllib.request
from pathlib import Path

# Box health belongs to the box-health lead's Envoy role (dispatch://INFRA-716), and to the SRE
# session when nobody holds that role.
BOX_HEALTH_ROLE_TOPIC = "notifications.role.lead-box-health"
SRE = "01a18385-f8c8-74a9-b6ff-694fb5fe87a7"

# Where the state file goes. /mnt/nvme/sami/tmp is the agent devboxes' scratch on the big disk;
# an agent box without it keeps the state under ~/.local/state, which every XDG machine has.
# Measured 2026-10-30 on ossv-agent-open..open6, ossv-agent-git and ossv-agent-repo: /mnt/nvme
# there is a root-owned 755 directory on the root filesystem with no sami/tmp under it, so this
# watch crashed on its first run of every one of them with PermissionError. The test is
# writability, not existence: the path can be a directory this user still cannot write into, and
# `--state` cannot be the answer, since a non-default state file forces --dry-run and the watch
# would then be armed and permanently silent.
_NVME_STATE = Path("/mnt/nvme/sami/tmp/omp-memory-watch/state.json")
DEFAULT_STATE = (
    _NVME_STATE
    if os.access("/mnt/nvme/sami/tmp", os.W_OK)
    else Path(os.environ.get("XDG_STATE_HOME", str(Path.home() / ".local/state"))) / "omp-memory-watch/state.json"
)
DEFAULT_ENVOY_URL = "http://127.0.0.1:9020"
SESSION_ROOT = Path.home() / ".omp/agent/sessions"
# Where this copy runs from. A VM runs it from a versioned path under /opt, never from the
# dotfiles checkout, and an alert that named the checkout sent its reader to the wrong file.
SELF_PATH = os.path.realpath(__file__)
CGROUP_ROOT = Path("/sys/fs/cgroup")
GIB = 1073741824

UUID_RE = re.compile(r"[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{12}")
# The session id of an omp is the id in the LAST path component of its --resume argument (the
# transcript file's own name), or of the transcript it holds open: never an id elsewhere in the
# path or argv. A lane resumed on sami-devbox from `…/<stamp>Z_01a15237-…/<stamp>Z_<own id>.jsonl`
# was named 01a15237 (its parent, which runs on sami-rocqperf) by the near-limit notice of
# 2026-11-03 23:07Z, because every id in the command line was read and the smallest one taken.
TRANSCRIPT_NAME_RE = re.compile(r"^(?:[0-9T:-]*Z_)?(" + UUID_RE.pattern + r")(?:\.jsonl)?$")
# A transcript's directory, `<stamp>Z_<id>`: the session whose subagents' transcripts it holds.
TRANSCRIPT_DIR_RE = re.compile(r"^[0-9T:-]*Z_(" + UUID_RE.pattern + r")$")
# A session's own memory self-watch, recognised by its command line. The known one is
# ol-rss-watch.sh; the pattern is deliberately wide, and a miss is reported as "could not tell",
# never as "has none".
SELF_WATCH_RE = re.compile(r"(?i)(rss|mem|memory|oom)[-_]?(watch|guard|watchdog|limit)")


def log(message: str) -> None:
    stamp = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime())
    print(f"{stamp} {message}", flush=True)


def iso(epoch: float | None) -> str:
    if not epoch:
        return "unknown"
    return time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime(epoch))


def human(seconds: float) -> str:
    seconds = int(seconds)
    if seconds < 90:
        return f"{seconds} s"
    minutes, rest = divmod(seconds, 60)
    if minutes < 90:
        return f"{minutes} min {rest} s"
    hours, minutes = divmod(minutes, 60)
    return f"{hours} h {minutes} min"


def gib(value: float) -> str:
    return f"{value / GIB:.2f} GiB"


# Every Envoy message this watch sends is at most MESSAGE_MAX characters and ends with
# `Full report: <path>`; the detail goes in that file (dispatch://INFRA-672). Measured on
# sami-rocqperf over 48 h to 2026-11-03: a NEAR alert was about 2 KB, a wall digest about 800
# characters, and the first ~200 characters of each already carried the decision.
MESSAGE_MAX = 600
REPORT_KEEP = 7 * 86400


def reports_dir(options: argparse.Namespace) -> Path:
    """`reports/` beside the state file: the watch's own directory."""
    return Path(options.state).parent / "reports"


def write_report(options: argparse.Namespace, kind: str, body: str, now: float) -> str:
    """Write one message's whole detail to reports/<UTC stamp>-<kind>.txt; return its path.

    A report that cannot be written is named as such, so the message never sends its reader to
    a file that is not there; the message itself still goes.
    """
    directory = reports_dir(options)
    stamp = time.strftime("%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ", time.gmtime(now))
    path = directory / f"{stamp}-{kind}.txt"
    try:
        directory.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        number = 2
        while path.exists():
            path, number = directory / f"{stamp}-{kind}-{number}.txt", number + 1
        path.write_text(body.rstrip("\n") + "\n")
    except OSError as error:
        log(f"  report {path} not written: {error}")
        return f"{path} (NOT WRITTEN: {error.strerror or error})"
    return str(path)


def prune_reports(options: argparse.Namespace, now: float) -> int:
    """Delete the reports older than REPORT_KEEP; return how many went."""
    try:
        entries = list(os.scandir(reports_dir(options)))
    except OSError:
        return 0
    gone = 0
    for entry in entries:
        try:
            if entry.is_file() and now - entry.stat().st_mtime > REPORT_KEEP:
                os.unlink(entry.path)
                gone += 1
        except OSError:
            continue
    return gone


def short_message(head: str, report: str) -> str:
    """`head`, then `Full report: <report>`, in at most MESSAGE_MAX characters.

    Each head is written to fit; the cut is the guarantee for an id, path or error text that is
    longer than expected.
    """
    tail = f"\nFull report: {report}"
    head = head.strip()
    room = MESSAGE_MAX - len(tail)
    if len(head) > room:
        head = head[: max(room - 3, 0)].rstrip() + "..."
    return head + tail


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- Envoy transport


class Envoy:
    """The listener this machine's sessions use, resolved the way scripts/envoy resolves it."""

    def __init__(self, timeout: float = 8.0) -> None:
        path = os.environ.get("ENVOY_CONFIG_FILE") or str(
            Path(os.environ.get("XDG_CONFIG_HOME", str(Path.home() / ".config")))
            / "legion/envoy.json"
        )
        config: dict = {}
        try:
            config = json.loads(Path(path).read_text()).get("envoy") or {}
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            config = {}
        if config.get("enabled") is False:
            raise SystemExit(f"{path} sets envoy.enabled to false: Envoy is off")
        self.url = (os.environ.get("ENVOY_URL") or config.get("url") or DEFAULT_ENVOY_URL).rstrip("/")
        self.timeout = timeout
        token = os.environ.get("ENVOY_TOKEN") or config.get("token") or ""
        if token.startswith("!"):
            try:
                done = subprocess.run(
                    ["/bin/sh", "-c", token[1:]],
                    capture_output=True,
                    text=True,
                    timeout=10,
                    stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                )
                token = done.stdout.strip() if done.returncode == 0 else ""
            except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
                token = ""
        self.token = token

    def request(self, method: str, path: str, body: dict | None = None) -> tuple[int, dict | list]:
        data = json.dumps(body).encode() if body is not None else None
        request = urllib.request.Request(self.url + path, data=data, method=method)
        if data is not None:
            request.add_header("Content-Type", "application/json")
        if self.token:
            request.add_header("Authorization", f"Bearer {self.token}")
        try:
            with urllib.request.urlopen(request, timeout=self.timeout) as answer:
                raw = answer.read().decode() or "{}"
                return answer.status, json.loads(raw)
        except urllib.error.HTTPError as error:
            raw = error.read().decode() or "{}"
            try:
                return error.code, json.loads(raw)
            except ValueError:
                return error.code, {"error": raw.strip()}
        except (urllib.error.URLError, socket.timeout, ValueError, OSError) as error:
            return 0, {"error": str(error)}

    def send(self, session_id: str, message: str) -> bool:
        status, body = self.request(
            "POST",
            "/v1/messages/send",
            {"target_session": session_id, "message": message, "source": "envoy"},
        )
        if status != 200:
            log(f"  send to {session_id} failed: {status} {body}")
        return status == 200

    def publish(self, topic: str, message: str) -> bool:
        status, body = self.request(
            "POST",
            "/v1/messages/publish",
            {"topic": topic, "message": message, "source": "envoy"},
        )
        if status != 200:
            log(f"  publish to {topic} failed: {status} {body}")
        return status == 200


# The sessions Envoy has live on THIS box, filled at most once per run and only when a wall
# report is about to name one. Empty with LIVE_READ False means "not looked up", which reads as
# "cannot verify" and never as "nothing is live".
#
# Why this watch needs it: a session id taken from an `omp --resume <path>` command line is the
# id the session directory was CREATED with, and a resume gets a new one. On ossv-agent-repo on
# 2026-10-30 that stale id was 01a14b36 (VERIFY, which runs on sami-rocqperf) while the live
# owner was 01a14b84 -- and load-swap-watch sent its 20:17:23Z alert to the wrong box's session
# for exactly that reason. A worker's own `.ossv-workers/<name>/session` file, which this watch
# prefers, follows a resume and does not have the defect; the fallback does.
LIVE_SESSIONS: dict[str, dict] = {}
LIVE_READ = False


def this_box(host: str) -> bool:
    """Whether an Envoy session row's host is this machine, by its first label."""
    if not host:
        return False
    mine = socket.gethostname()
    return host == mine or host.split(".")[0] == mine.split(".")[0]


def refresh_live_sessions(envoy: Envoy) -> None:
    """Ask Envoy which sessions are live on this box. One GET, at most once per run."""
    global LIVE_READ
    status, body = envoy.request("GET", "/v1/sessions")
    if status != 200 or not isinstance(body, list):
        log(f"  live sessions unavailable: GET /v1/sessions answered {status}; attribution unverified")
        return
    LIVE_SESSIONS.clear()
    for row in body:
        if isinstance(row, dict) and row.get("session_id") and this_box(str(row.get("host") or "")):
            LIVE_SESSIONS[str(row["session_id"])] = row
    LIVE_READ = True
    log(f"  {len(LIVE_SESSIONS)} session(s) live on this box, of {len(body)} Envoy knows")


def verify_touch_session(envoy: Envoy, touch: dict) -> None:
    """Check the touch's session id against what is live here, and retarget when it is wrong.

    A report sent to a session id that is not live on this box does not reach a silent owner:
    it reaches whoever holds that id somewhere else. So a claim that cannot be verified here is
    replaced by the live owner when one can be named, and the report says what happened.
    """
    if not LIVE_READ:
        refresh_live_sessions(envoy)
    claim = touch.get("session") or ""
    if not LIVE_READ:
        touch["session_note"] = ""
        return
    if claim and claim in LIVE_SESSIONS:
        touch["session_note"] = ""
        return
    # A lane resumed from a subagent's transcript: its session is not registered, its parent is.
    route_lane(envoy, touch)
    if touch["lane_of"]:
        touch["session_note"] = "\n" + lane_note(touch)
        return
    owner = ""
    if touch.get("worker_session") and touch["worker_session"] in LIVE_SESSIONS:
        owner = touch["worker_session"]
    elif len(LIVE_SESSIONS) == 1:
        owner = next(iter(LIVE_SESSIONS))
    touch["session_claim"] = claim
    touch["session"] = owner
    if claim and owner:
        touch["session_note"] = (
            f" The id {claim} on this scope's processes is not live on this box -- an"
            " `omp --resume` keeps the session directory's original id, and an inherited"
            f" OMP_SESSION_ID names whoever started the box -- so this went to {owner}, the live"
            " owner here."
        )
    elif claim:
        touch["session_note"] = (
            f" The id {claim} on this scope's processes is not live on this box and no live"
            " session here could be matched to it, so no session was told: the box-health owner"
            " hears of it instead."
        )
    else:
        touch["session_note"] = ""


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ this machine's omp scopes


def read_int(path: Path) -> int | None:
    try:
        text = path.read_text().strip()
    except OSError:
        return None
    if text == "max":
        return None
    try:
        return int(text)
    except ValueError:
        return None


def mem_total() -> int:
    try:
        for line in Path("/proc/meminfo").read_text().splitlines():
            if line.startswith("MemTotal:"):
                return int(line.split()[1]) * 1024
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
        pass
    return 0


def cgroup_of(pid: int) -> str:
    """The cgroup v2 path of a pid, as written in /proc/<pid>/cgroup ("0::/user.slice/...")."""
    try:
        for line in (PROC_ROOT / str(pid) / "cgroup").read_text().splitlines():
            parts = line.split(":", 2)
            if len(parts) == 3 and parts[0] == "0":
                return parts[2].strip()
    except OSError:
        pass
    return ""


def effective_limit(cgroup: str) -> tuple[int, str, bool]:
    """(ceiling in bytes, where it comes from, whether the session has a cap of its OWN).

    The ceiling is the smallest numeric `memory.max` from this cgroup up to the root. A scope
    started by `shims/omp` carries MemoryMax, and since 2026-10-27 `user-1000.slice` carries a
    box-level one too (installers/omp-box-memory-guard.sh): 232 GiB, so the user's sessions
    together can never take the RAM the system's daemons need.

    The third value is whether THIS cgroup -- not an ancestor -- carries the limit. That is
    what decides how a session is measured: a session in a scope of its own is measured on the
    scope, while one that only inherits the slice's ceiling shares that ceiling with every
    other session in the login and is measured on its own process instead. Before the slice
    had a ceiling the two tests were the same; with one, charging a session the whole login
    scope's memory against a 232 GiB slice would warn on nothing at all.
    """
    best: int | None = None
    where = ""
    parts = [p for p in cgroup.strip("/").split("/") if p]
    path = CGROUP_ROOT
    for part in [""] + parts:
        path = path / part if part else path
        value = read_int(path / "memory.max")
        if value is not None and (best is None or value < best):
            best, where = value, part or "(cgroup root)"
    own = read_int(CGROUP_ROOT / cgroup.strip("/") / "memory.max") is not None
    if best is None:
        return mem_total(), "no cgroup limit anywhere in the chain: the machine's RAM", False
    return best, f"memory.max of {where}", own


def scope_pressure(cgroup: str) -> tuple[int, int, dict[str, int]]:
    """(unreclaimable bytes, memory.current, the memory.stat fields behind them).

    Unreclaimable is anon + shmem + kernel + sock. In a cgroup that `shims/omp` starts with
    MemorySwapMax=0 that is exact: its anonymous memory cannot be paged out even though the box
    now has swap, so file cache is the only part of memory.current the kernel can give back, and
    counting cache towards the ceiling would alert on a scope in no danger. A caller measuring a
    cgroup that CAN swap (a slice with no MemorySwapMax) gets a conservative over-estimate of the
    danger, which is the safe direction for a guard.
    """
    directory = CGROUP_ROOT / cgroup.strip("/")
    current = read_int(directory / "memory.current") or 0
    fields: dict[str, int] = {}
    try:
        for line in (directory / "memory.stat").read_text().splitlines():
            key, _, value = line.partition(" ")
            if key in ("anon", "file", "kernel", "sock", "shmem", "slab"):
                fields[key] = int(value)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return current, current, fields
    unreclaimable = fields.get("anon", 0) + fields.get("shmem", 0) + fields.get("kernel", 0) + fields.get("sock", 0)
    return min(unreclaimable, current), current, fields


def memory_events(cgroup: str) -> dict[str, int]:
    """The scope's memory.events counters, keyed by name ("max", "oom", "oom_kill", ...).

    `max` counts the times the kernel refused an allocation at memory.max and had to reclaim to
    satisfy it: a scope whose `max` rises has REACHED its wall, whatever its unreclaimable
    memory reads. It is the only signal that does not need the pressure to be held: a worker
    that goes from comfortable to the ceiling and back inside one 30 s period leaves no other
    trace short of an OOM kill.
    """
    counters: dict[str, int] = {}
    try:
        for line in (CGROUP_ROOT / cgroup.strip("/") / "memory.events").read_text().splitlines():
            key, _, value = line.partition(" ")
            try:
                counters[key] = int(value)
            except ValueError:
                continue
    except OSError:
        pass
    return counters


def pressure_line(cgroup: str) -> str:
    """One plain line from the scope's memory.pressure (cgroup v2 PSI), read now.

    The librarian's eval harness asked for it (2026-11-04 12:25Z): an eval arm ran near its
    21.91 GiB ceiling at 11:51-12:01Z and then ended at its time limit after its cgroup was
    gone, and no report held its stall totals, so nobody could say afterwards whether it had
    stalled. A file that cannot be read is named as such; it never fails the report.
    """
    read_at = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime())
    if not cgroup:
        return "memory.pressure not readable: no cgroup recorded for this scope"
    try:
        text = (CGROUP_ROOT / cgroup.strip("/") / "memory.pressure").read_text()
    except OSError as error:
        return f"memory.pressure not readable: {error.strerror or error}"
    parts = []
    for line in text.splitlines():
        kind, _, rest = line.partition(" ")
        fields = dict(item.split("=", 1) for item in rest.split() if "=" in item)
        try:
            parts.append(
                f"{kind} avg60 {float(fields['avg60']):.2f}%, total {int(fields['total']) / 1e6:.1f} s"
            )
        except (KeyError, ValueError):
            continue
    if not parts:
        return f"memory.pressure not readable: no some/full lines in {text.strip()[:80]!r}"
    return f"Memory pressure (memory.pressure at {read_at}): " + "; ".join(parts)



def swap_setting(cgroup: str) -> str:
    """The scope's memory.swap.max as written ("0", "max", a byte count), or "" when unreadable.

    The alert says a scope's anonymous memory cannot be paged out only when this reads 0. A
    scope `shims/omp` starts carries MemorySwapMax=0 and a scope `ossv-worker` starts does not,
    and the alert used to claim the first of both.
    """
    try:
        return (CGROUP_ROOT / cgroup.strip("/") / "memory.swap.max").read_text().strip()
    except OSError:
        return ""


def swap_sentence(swap_max: str) -> str:
    """What this run can truthfully say about the scope's swap, from memory.swap.max alone."""
    if swap_max == "0":
        return " This scope's memory.swap.max is 0, so the rest it cannot page out."
    if swap_max == "max":
        return (
            " This scope's memory.swap.max is `max`, so its anonymous memory CAN be paged out to"
            " the box's swap -- which is why its unreclaimable number can sit low while the scope"
            " still reaches its wall."
        )
    if swap_max:
        return f" This scope's memory.swap.max is {swap_max}, so some of the rest can be paged out."
    return " This scope's memory.swap.max could not be read, so nothing is claimed about its swap."


# A worker's name, as `ossv-worker` writes it into every path under its state root: the omp
# process of worker `c-asan` is started with `@<root>/.ossv-workers/c-asan/message-resume-0.md`
# and works under `<home>/work/workers/c-asan/`. Taking it from the command line means no path
# is hard-coded here and the directory it names can be read for the worker's session id.
# `\S*` would swallow omp's `@` prompt-file prefix into the path, so the directory starts at a
# `/` and holds no `@`.
WORKER_RE = re.compile(r"(?P<dir>(?P<root>/[^\s@]*?/\.ossv-workers)/(?P<name>[^/\s]+))(?:/|\s|$)")


def worker_of(pids: list[int]) -> tuple[str, str]:
    """(worker name, its state directory) for the first process in the scope that names one."""
    for pid in pids:
        found = WORKER_RE.search(cmdline_of(pid))
        if found:
            return found.group("name"), found.group("dir")
    return "", ""


def worker_session(directory: str) -> str:
    """The session id `ossv-worker` recorded for this worker, when it has confirmed one.

    `session_unconfirmed` beside it means `$d/session` still names the PREVIOUS session (the
    worker's own rule), so it is not used then: a wrong session id sends an owner to checkpoint
    a session that is not theirs.
    """
    if not directory:
        return ""
    try:
        if (Path(directory) / "session_unconfirmed").exists():
            return ""
        found = UUID_RE.findall((Path(directory) / "session").read_text())
    except OSError:
        return ""
    return found[0] if found else ""


# What the memory.stat fields mean to the reader of an alert. `kernel` already covers `slab`,
# so slab is named only to say how much of the kernel number it is.
STAT_MEANING = {
    "anon": "anonymous memory the processes allocated (heaps, stacks)",
    "file": "page cache: files the scope read or wrote",
    "shmem": "shared memory and tmpfs, which is charged to the scope that touches it",
    "kernel": "kernel memory charged to the scope (slab, page tables, kernel stacks)",
    "slab": "kernel slab, part of the kernel number",
    "sock": "socket buffers",
}
# The lever that field answers to. A generic "reduce your file traffic" under a scope whose
# memory is anonymous is advice for a different scope, so each field names its own.
STAT_LEVER = {
    "anon": " Anonymous memory IS process memory, so a low RSS sum with a high anon number means"
    " the processes that held it have already ended: look at what this lane ran, not at what it"
    " runs now.",
    "file": " Page cache is charged to the scope that reads or writes the file. The lever is this"
    " lane's file traffic -- big checkouts, build trees, logs, archives -- not its lane count.",
    "shmem": " tmpfs and shared memory stay charged until they are deleted. Look for what this"
    " lane wrote under /dev/shm or another tmpfs and never removed.",
    "kernel": " Kernel memory grows with the number of processes, mappings and open files, so the"
    " lever is how many of those this lane keeps at once, not how large any one of them is.",
    "sock": " Socket buffers this large mean a stalled or very fast stream: look at what this"
    " lane is downloading or piping.",
}
# A scope is one runaway process when the named processes hold this much of its peak, and a
# crowd when every process together does. Between and below them the memory is not in any
# process's RSS at all, which is what the memory.stat split answers.
RUNAWAY_SHARE = 0.60
CROWD_SHARE = 0.70


def stat_split(stats: dict) -> str:
    """The memory.stat fields of the scope, largest first, as one line."""
    rows = [(name, int(value)) for name, value in (stats or {}).items() if int(value) > 0 and name != "slab"]
    if not rows:
        return "memory.stat unreadable"
    rows.sort(key=lambda row: row[1], reverse=True)
    return ", ".join(f"{name} {gib(value)}" for name, value in rows)


def dominant_stat(stats: dict) -> tuple[str, int]:
    """The largest memory.stat field and its size, ignoring `slab` (it is inside `kernel`)."""
    rows = [(name, int(value)) for name, value in (stats or {}).items() if name != "slab"]
    if not rows:
        return "", 0
    return max(rows, key=lambda row: row[1])


def wall_advice(touch: dict) -> str:
    """What the owner should do, decided by where the scope's memory actually is.

    Telling an owner to hunt a process is wrong when no process holds the memory: in the open4
    and open6 digests of 2026-10-30 the three largest processes held 10-29% of a scope sitting
    at its ceiling, and the rest was page cache the owner's own file traffic had charged there.
    """
    peak = max(int(touch.get("peak") or 0), 1)
    top_sum = sum(rss for _pid, rss, _command in touch.get("top") or [])
    rss_sum = int(touch.get("rss_sum") or 0)
    count = int(touch.get("task_count") or 0)
    current = int(touch.get("current") or 0)
    if top_sum >= RUNAWAY_SHARE * peak:
        return (
            f"ONE RUNAWAY: the {len(touch.get('top') or [])} processes named above hold"
            f" {gib(top_sum)}, {100 * top_sum / peak:.0f}% of the peak. Find that process and"
            " stop or bound it -- shedding lanes will not help while it runs."
        )
    if rss_sum >= CROWD_SHARE * peak:
        return (
            f"MANY PROCESSES: no single one is large, but all {count} together hold"
            f" {gib(rss_sum)}, {100 * rss_sum / peak:.0f}% of the peak. Reduce the fan-out --"
            " run fewer lanes or fewer tool subprocesses at a time."
        )
    name, size = dominant_stat(touch.get("stats") or {})
    fell_back = current < peak * 0.5
    if not name:
        where = " memory.stat could not be read, so this run cannot say where it is."
    else:
        where = (
            f" The largest field of its memory.stat{' now' if fell_back else ''} is {name} at"
            f" {gib(size)}: {STAT_MEANING.get(name, 'see memory.stat')}.{STAT_LEVER.get(name, '')}"
        )
    if fell_back:
        where += (
            f" The scope has since fallen back to {gib(current)} of memory.current, so the peak"
            " was a burst: nothing on it now holds that memory, and the split above describes"
            " the scope as it is now, not as it was at the ceiling. Look at what this lane had"
            f" just started when it touched the wall at {iso(touch.get('at'))}."
        )
    return (
        f"NOT IN ANY PROCESS: all {count} processes together hold only {gib(rss_sum)},"
        f" {100 * rss_sum / peak:.0f}% of the peak, so hunting a process is the wrong move."
        + where
    )


def omp_processes() -> list[tuple[int, str]]:
    """Every `omp` process on this machine, as (pid, its cgroup path)."""
    found = []
    for entry in os.listdir("/proc"):
        if not entry.isdigit():
            continue
        pid = int(entry)
        try:
            if Path(f"/proc/{pid}/comm").read_text().strip() != "omp":
                continue
        except OSError:
            continue
        cgroup = cgroup_of(pid)
        if cgroup:
            found.append((pid, cgroup))
    return found


def rss_of(pid: int) -> int:
    """VmRSS in bytes, read from /proc/<pid>/statm (resident pages)."""
    try:
        return int((PROC_ROOT / str(pid) / "statm").read_text().split()[1]) * os.sysconf("SC_PAGE_SIZE")
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
        return 0


def pid_start(pid: int) -> int:
    """Field 22 of /proc/<pid>/stat: the clock ticks since boot at which the process started.

    pids on this box wrap inside a day, so an incident remembers this with the pid and starts
    over when it changes -- a remembered pid alone can come back as an unrelated process.
    """
    try:
        data = (PROC_ROOT / str(pid) / "stat").read_text()
        return int(data[data.rfind(")") + 2 :].split()[19])
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
        return 0


def scope_tasks(cgroup: str) -> list[int]:
    try:
        return [int(line) for line in (CGROUP_ROOT / cgroup.strip("/") / "cgroup.procs").read_text().split()]
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return []


def cmdline_of(pid: int) -> str:
    try:
        return Path(f"/proc/{pid}/cmdline").read_bytes().decode("utf8", "replace").replace("\0", " ").strip()
    except OSError:
        return ""


def resume_value(words: list[str]) -> str:
    """The argument of the `--resume`/`--restore` word of an omp's argv, else ""."""
    for i, word in enumerate(words):
        flag, eq, value = word.partition("=")
        if flag in ("--resume", "--restore"):
            return value if eq else (words[i + 1] if i + 1 < len(words) else "")
    return ""


def resume_session(words: list[str]) -> str:
    """The session an omp's argv resumes: the id in the last path component of its --resume."""
    found = TRANSCRIPT_NAME_RE.match(resume_value(words).rstrip("/").rsplit("/", 1)[-1])
    return found.group(1) if found else ""


def held_transcripts(pid: int) -> list[str]:
    """The session transcripts an omp holds open, by path."""
    held = []
    try:
        for descriptor in os.listdir(f"/proc/{pid}/fd"):
            try:
                target = os.readlink(f"/proc/{pid}/fd/{descriptor}")
            except OSError:
                continue
            if "/.omp/agent/sessions/" in target and TRANSCRIPT_NAME_RE.match(os.path.basename(target)):
                held.append(target)
    except OSError:
        pass
    return sorted(held)


def session_of(pid: int) -> str:
    """The session id an omp process runs: the last path component of its --resume, else the
    name of the transcript it holds open. Never another id on its command line: a resumed lane's
    path names its parent first, and a prompt argument can hold any id.

    Resolved only for a process that is about to be alerted on, which is what keeps a run to
    a handful of file reads: the fd scan is the expensive part.
    """
    session = resume_session(cmdline_of(pid).split())
    if session:
        return session
    found = sorted({TRANSCRIPT_NAME_RE.match(os.path.basename(path)).group(1) for path in held_transcripts(pid)})
    return found[0] if found else ""


def scope_session(pids: list[int], first: int) -> tuple[str, int]:
    """(session id, omp pid) of a capped scope: its largest omp's, else any other omp's in it.

    The largest omp process in a scope is not always the session. On 2026-11-02 at 09:41:57Z the
    librarian's scope reached its wall led by omp's worker broker (`omp __omp_worker_daemon_broker`),
    which names no session and holds no transcript, so the report went to nobody while the
    session's own omp sat beside it in the same scope.
    """
    session = session_of(first)
    if session:
        return session, first
    for pid in pids:
        command = cmdline_of(pid)
        if pid == first or not command or "__omp_worker" in command or Path(command.split()[0]).name != "omp":
            continue
        session = session_of(pid)
        if session:
            return session, pid
    return "", first


def omp_identity(pid: int) -> dict:
    """Who a notice is about, named so its reader can tell a lane from itself: the omp's pid, its
    working directory, its tmux window, and the session whose transcript directory holds its
    transcript (`…/<stamp>Z_<parent>/<stamp>Z_<own>.jsonl`), if any."""
    try:
        cwd = os.readlink(f"/proc/{pid}/cwd")
    except OSError:
        cwd = ""
    path = resume_value(cmdline_of(pid).split())
    if "/" not in path:
        held = held_transcripts(pid)
        path = held[0] if len(held) == 1 else ""
    parent = TRANSCRIPT_DIR_RE.match(os.path.basename(os.path.dirname(path))) if path else None
    env = dict(word.split("=", 1) for word in proc_words(pid, "environ") if "=" in word)
    window = ""
    if env.get("TMUX") and env.get("TMUX_PANE"):
        try:
            done = subprocess.run(
                ["tmux", "-S", env["TMUX"].split(",")[0], "display-message", "-p", "-t", env["TMUX_PANE"],
                 "#{session_name}:#{window_index} (#{window_name})"],
                capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=3, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
            )
            window = done.stdout.strip() if done.returncode == 0 else ""
        except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
            window = ""
        window = window or f"pane {env['TMUX_PANE']}"
    return {"omp_pid": pid, "cwd": cwd, "window": window, "transcript_parent": parent.group(1) if parent else ""}


def identity_phrase(record: dict) -> str:
    """`omp pid P, tmux W, cwd C`: the scope's own omp, as every notice names it."""
    parts = [f"omp pid {record.get('omp_pid') or record.get('pid') or '?'}"]
    if record.get("window"):
        parts.append(f"tmux {record['window']}")
    parts.append(f"cwd {record.get('cwd') or '(unreadable)'}")
    return ", ".join(parts)


def route_lane(envoy: "Envoy", record: dict) -> None:
    """A session with no live Envoy registration here, whose transcript sits in a live session's
    directory, is that session's lane: the notice goes to the parent, marked as a lane. Sets
    record["lane_of"]; changes nothing when Envoy cannot be read or the session is live."""
    record["lane_of"] = ""
    session, parent = record.get("session") or "", record.get("transcript_parent") or ""
    if not session or not parent or parent == session:
        return
    if not LIVE_READ:
        refresh_live_sessions(envoy)
    if LIVE_READ and session not in LIVE_SESSIONS and parent in LIVE_SESSIONS:
        record["lane_of"] = parent


def lane_note(record: dict) -> str:
    """The sentence a parent reads first when it is told of its lane."""
    if not record.get("lane_of"):
        return ""
    return (
        f"A LANE OF YOURS, NOT YOU: {identity_phrase(record)} runs session {record['session']}, whose"
        f" transcript is in your session's directory; it has no live Envoy registration, so you are told."
    )


def self_watch_anywhere(omp_pid: int) -> str:
    """The self-watch test for a session with no scope of its own.

    Scope membership says nothing there (the login scope holds every session in the login), so
    the test is a process anywhere on this machine whose command line both reads like a memory
    watch and names this omp pid as an argument -- which is how the one known self-watch is
    started (`ol-rss-watch.sh <omp pid> 10`).
    """
    for entry in os.listdir("/proc"):
        if not entry.isdigit() or int(entry) == omp_pid:
            continue
        command = cmdline_of(int(entry))
        if command and SELF_WATCH_RE.search(command) and str(omp_pid) in command.split():
            return f"yes -- pid {entry} runs `{command[:160]}`"
    return (
        "could not tell: no process on this machine has a memory-watch command line naming pid "
        f"{omp_pid}. It may run one in-session (a tool loop this check cannot see), or it may "
        "have none and this is its only warning"
    )


def self_watch_of(cgroup: str, omp_pid: int) -> str:
    """What this run can say about the session's OWN memory self-watch.

    The session owns prevention; this watch only detects. A session that runs its own watch
    has already been warned before this one fires, and one that does not has only this. The
    test is a command-line match on the other processes in the same scope, so it can miss a
    self-watch that runs elsewhere -- which the alert says, rather than implying there is none.
    """
    for pid in scope_tasks(cgroup):
        if pid == omp_pid:
            continue
        command = cmdline_of(pid)
        if command and SELF_WATCH_RE.search(command):
            return f"yes -- pid {pid} in the same scope runs `{command[:160]}`"
    return (
        "could not tell: no process in this scope has a memory-watch command line. It may run "
        "one in-session (a tool loop this check cannot see), or it may have none and this is "
        "its only warning"
    )


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- the decision


class State(object):
    """The one file this watch owns: per-scope samples and the open crossings.

    Opened under an exclusive, non-blocking flock for the whole run, so a hand run and the
    timer can never both decide that a crossing is new -- the loser skips its period. `commit`
    writes the whole file and fsyncs it, and every alert commits before it sends, so the
    once-per-crossing record is on disk before the message exists.
    """

    def __init__(self, path: Path) -> None:
        self.path = path
        path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        self.handle = open(path, "a+")
        try:
            fcntl.flock(self.handle, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
            self.locked = True
        except OSError:
            self.locked = False
            self.data = {}
            return
        self.handle.seek(0)
        try:
            self.data = json.loads(self.handle.read() or "{}")
        except ValueError:
            self.data = {}

    def commit(self, options: argparse.Namespace) -> None:
        if options.dry_run and not options.write_state:
            return
        self.handle.seek(0)
        self.handle.truncate()
        json.dump(self.data, self.handle, indent=1, sort_keys=True)
        self.handle.flush()
        os.fsync(self.handle.fileno())

    def close(self) -> None:
        try:
            self.handle.close()
        except OSError:
            pass


def rate_per_min(samples: list[list[float]]) -> float | None:
    """GiB per minute across the samples kept for this scope, or None with too few."""
    if len(samples) < 2:
        return None
    span = samples[-1][0] - samples[0][0]
    if span < 30:
        return None
    return (samples[-1][1] - samples[0][1]) / GIB / (span / 60)


def alert_text(scope: str, incident: dict, now: float, kind: str) -> str:
    limit = incident["limit"]
    warn_at = incident["warn_at"]
    usage = incident["peak"]
    headroom = limit - usage
    rate = incident.get("rate")
    session = incident.get("session") or ""
    pid = incident.get("pid")
    limited = incident.get("limited", True)
    eta = ""
    if rate is not None:
        eta = f" It has risen at {rate:.2f} GiB/min over the last {human(incident.get('rate_span') or 0)}."
        if rate > 0.05 and limited:
            # Only a capped session has a headroom worth converting into minutes: for an
            # uncapped one the "ceiling" is the whole machine, shared with every other session.
            eta = (
                f" At the {rate:.2f} GiB/min it has risen at over the last"
                f" {human(incident.get('rate_span') or 0)}, that headroom is"
                f" {headroom / GIB / rate:.0f} minutes."
            )
    # The session is read from the PROCESS -- its command line, else the transcript it holds
    # open -- never from who is sending or reading this. An alert that guessed would send a
    # lead to checkpoint the wrong session.
    who = (
        f"Session {session}"
        if session
        else "Session unknown (this process names no session id on its command line and holds no"
        " session transcript open)"
    )
    memory = (
        f"Memory: {gib(usage)} unreclaimable (anon+shmem+kernel+sock) in its scope, of a"
        f" {gib(limit)} ceiling -- {100 * usage / limit:.0f}%, {gib(headroom)} left. The ceiling"
        f" is {incident.get('limit_from')}. The scope's memory.current is"
        f" {gib(incident.get('current') or 0)}; the difference is file cache, which the kernel can"
        f" reclaim.{swap_sentence(incident.get('swap_max') or '')}{eta}"
        if limited
        else f"Memory: this omp process holds {gib(usage)} RSS. {gib(limit)} is the ceiling --"
        f" {incident.get('limit_from')} -- and {gib(warn_at)} is "
        + (f"{100 * warn_at / max(limit, 1):.0f}% of it, the share this watch warns at"
           if limit < mem_total()
           else "the level a CAPPED session is warned at")
        + f", which this one has passed.{eta}"
    )
    tasks = incident.get("task_count") or 1
    composition = (
        f"The scope holds {tasks} process{'es' if tasks != 1 else ''}; the omp process is"
        f" {100 * (incident.get('omp_rss') or 0) / max(usage, 1):.0f}% of the pressure"
        + (", its tool subprocesses the rest" if tasks > 1 else "")
        + ". The kernel OOM-kills on the SCOPE, not on omp's RSS: at the 05:55:14Z kill omp"
        " held 14.9 GiB of the 24 GiB that hit the ceiling."
        if limited
        else "This session has NO scope of its own, so nothing caps it and its tool subprocesses"
        f" are not counted here -- only omp's own RSS is. Its login scope {scope.split(':')[0]}"
        " is shared with every other session in that login, so what kills it is the machine"
        " running out, which takes the box with it."
    )
    lines = [
        f"OMP SESSION NEAR ITS MEMORY LIMIT ({kind}): {who} has been over"
        f" {gib(warn_at)} for {human(now - (incident.get('opened_at') or now))}, first detected at"
        f" {iso(incident.get('opened_at'))}.",
        f"Process: omp pid {incident.get('omp_pid') or pid} (RSS {gib(incident.get('omp_rss') or 0)}) in scope"
        f" {incident.get('scope') or scope} on {socket.gethostname()}"
        + (f", tmux {incident['window']}" if incident.get("window") else ", in no tmux pane it could read")
        + f", cwd {incident.get('cwd') or '(unreadable)'}. Its session id is the name of its own"
        " transcript (the last path component of its --resume, else the file it holds open)"
        + (f"; that transcript is in session {incident['transcript_parent']}'s directory"
           if incident.get("transcript_parent") else "")
        + f". Measured: {incident.get('measured') or 'unrecorded'}.",
        memory,
        pressure_line(incident.get("pressure_cgroup") or incident.get("cgroup") or ""),
        composition,
        f"Your own self-watch: {incident.get('self_watch')}.",
        "DO, on your own terms and before the kernel decides for you: write your handover files"
        " now (what each lane was doing, where its work is); stop spawning lanes; shed or finish"
        " the lanes you can; then restart yourself with `omp --resume <your session id>` in your"
        " tmux pane while your state is on disk. An OOM kill loses every in-flight lane with no"
        " exit record -- about 40 of them on 2026-10-27T05:55:14Z.",
        "This watch only detects. It kills nothing, sheds nothing and restarts nothing: the"
        " shedding is yours, on your own terms.",
        f"From omp-memory-watch ({SELF_PATH}) on "
        f"{socket.gethostname()}, a systemd --user timer every 30 s.",
    ]
    return "\n".join(lines)


def near_where(key: str, incident: dict) -> str:
    """`session <id>, scope <scope> on <box>`: who and where, as every crossing message opens."""
    session = incident.get("session") or "unknown"
    return (f"session {session} ({identity_phrase(incident)}), scope {incident.get('scope') or key}"
            f" on {socket.gethostname()}")


def near_level(incident: dict, usage: int) -> str:
    """How close: the measured number against its ceiling, and what is left."""
    limit = max(int(incident.get("limit") or 0), 1)
    if not incident.get("limited", True):
        # A session in an agent box has no scope of ITS OWN, and its box's memory.max is still a
        # real wall: 24 GiB for box agentbox-a4892446 on sami-devbox at 04:46Z on 2026-11-04,
        # where the machine has 2.1 TiB. "Only the box's RAM caps it" told such a session nothing
        # capped it, and the 16.8 GiB it names is 70% of that 24 GiB, not a flat level.
        if limit < mem_total():
            return (
                f"its omp holds {gib(usage)} RSS of the {gib(limit)} its box allows"
                f" ({100 * usage / limit:.0f}%, {gib(limit - usage)} left; the ceiling is"
                f" {incident.get('limit_from') or 'the box'}), with no scope of its own inside it"
            )
        return (
            f"its omp holds {gib(usage)} RSS with no scope of its own, past the"
            f" {gib(incident.get('warn_at') or 0)} a capped session is warned at; only the machine's RAM caps it"
        )
    return (
        f"{gib(usage)} unreclaimable of its {gib(limit)} ceiling ({100 * usage / limit:.0f}%,"
        f" {gib(limit - usage)} left)"
    )


def near_head(key: str, incident: dict, now: float, crossing: int) -> str:
    """The session's first alert: who, how close, how fast, and what to do, in one paragraph."""
    usage = int(incident.get("usage") or incident.get("peak") or 0)
    rate = incident.get("rate")
    pace = ""
    if rate is not None and rate > 0.05 and incident.get("limited", True):
        left = int(incident.get("limit") or 0) - usage
        pace = f"; rising {rate:.2f} GiB/min, about {left / GIB / rate:.0f} min of headroom"
    head = (
        (lane_note(incident) + " " if incident.get("lane_of") else "")
        + f"OMP SESSION NEAR ITS MEMORY LIMIT: {near_where(key, incident)}: {near_level(incident, usage)};"
        f" over {gib(incident.get('warn_at') or 0)} for {human(now - (incident.get('opened_at') or now))}{pace}."
    )
    if crossing >= 2:
        head += f" Crossing {crossing} for this session."
    if incident.get("lane_of"):
        head += (
            f" DO now: check on that lane (pid {incident.get('omp_pid')}); have it write its handover"
            " and shed, or stop it. Do not restart yourself for it. This watch kills nothing."
        )
    elif incident.get("session"):
        head += (
            " DO now: write handover files, stop spawning lanes, shed what you can, then"
            " `omp --resume <your id>` in your pane. This watch kills nothing."
        )
    else:
        head += (
            " No session could be named from its processes, so the box-health owner hears it: find"
            " who runs this scope (the report names its omp pid)."
        )
    return head


def rising_head(key: str, incident: dict, usage: int, now: float) -> str:
    """The box-health owner's copy: the session was told and the scope kept rising."""
    return (
        f"OMP SESSION STILL RISING {human(now - incident['alerted_at'])} AFTER ITS MEMORY ALERT:"
        f" {near_where(key, incident)}: {gib(incident.get('alert_usage') or 0)} at the alert"
        f" ({iso(incident['alerted_at'])}), now {near_level(incident, usage)}; nobody has shed."
        " Check that the session is alive and shedding, or tell its lead."
    )


def oom_head(key: str, incident: dict, before: int, after: int) -> str:
    """The box-health owner's copy: the kernel OOM-killed in a scope its session was warned about."""
    return (
        f"OMP SCOPE OOM KILL DURING A MEMORY ALERT: the kernel killed {after - before} process(es)"
        f" in {near_where(key, incident)} (memory.events oom_kill {before} -> {after}). Its session"
        f" was told it was near its limit at {iso(incident.get('alerted_at'))}. Check whether the"
        " session's omp still runs; the report has the alert it was sent."
    )


def reclaimable_bytes(touch: dict) -> int | None:
    """The part of this scope's charge the kernel can give back, or None when it cannot be told.

    memory.current minus the unreclaimable part (anon+shmem+kernel+sock) is page cache: clean
    file pages the kernel drops under pressure, and dirty ones it writes back and then drops.
    None means memory.stat was unreadable, where `scope_pressure` counts the whole of
    memory.current as unreclaimable and no split exists to show.
    """
    if not (touch.get("stats") or {}):
        return None
    return max(int(touch.get("current") or 0) - int(touch.get("usage") or 0), 0)


def reclaimable_clause(touch: dict) -> str:
    """The headline's one clause: how much of the ceiling is page cache the kernel can drop.

    It decides whether the rest of the report means anything, so it goes in the first sentence
    and not three paragraphs down beside the memory.stat split.
    """
    limit = max(int(touch.get("limit") or 0), 1)
    free = reclaimable_bytes(touch)
    if free is None:
        return ", of which none can be shown reclaimable: its memory.stat could not be read"
    return f", of which {gib(free)} ({100 * free / limit:.0f}% of the ceiling) is reclaimable page cache"


# Why the advice below turns on oom_kill, oom and the unreclaimable share, and not on the fact
# of being at the wall. From Documentation/admin-guide/cgroup-v2.rst (read 2026-11-01 at
# https://www.kernel.org/doc/Documentation/admin-guide/cgroup-v2.rst):
#
#   memory.max  "If a cgroup's memory usage reaches this limit and can't be reduced, the OOM
#               killer is invoked in the cgroup."
#   max         "The number of times the cgroup's memory usage was about to go over the max
#               boundary.  If direct reclaim fails to bring it down, the cgroup goes to OOM
#               state."
#   oom         "The number of time the cgroup's memory usage was reached the limit and
#               allocation was about to fail."
#   oom_kill    "The number of processes belonging to this cgroup killed by any kind of OOM
#               killer."
#   file        "Amount of memory used to cache filesystem data, including tmpfs and shared
#               memory."
#
# So `max` rising is the kernel SUCCEEDING: usage reached the boundary, direct reclaim brought
# it down, the allocation went through. The counter that rises when reclaim FAILS is `oom`, and
# the one that rises when something dies is `oom_kill`. The kill condition the kernel documents
# is "can't be reduced", and a scope holding gigabytes of page cache can be reduced. A scope
# whose ceiling is mostly cache therefore produces `max` events in normal operation and can sit
# at memory.max indefinitely; telling its owner it is one allocation from death is false, and
# the shedding that advice provokes -- killed builds, halved gates, abandoned measurements --
# costs more than the event does.
#
# The urgent side keeps every shape `urgent_reason` already escalates on, plus `oom` above zero
# and the unreclaimable share test, so this verdict is urgent wherever the box-health owner is
# told at once and the two can never contradict each other. Nothing here changes WHETHER a
# report is sent: every threshold that gates a report is untouched.


def wall_state(touch: dict) -> tuple[str, str]:
    """("urgent" or "normal", the sentence saying which and why, from this scope's counters)."""
    state, _short, verdict = wall_verdict(touch)
    return state, verdict


def wall_verdict(touch: dict) -> tuple[str, str, str]:
    """("urgent" or "normal", the verdict in one short sentence for the message, the whole of it)."""
    limit = max(int(touch.get("limit") or 0), 1)
    peak = max(int(touch.get("peak") or 0), 1)
    usage = int(touch.get("usage") or 0)
    anon = int((touch.get("stats") or {}).get("anon", 0))
    oom = int(touch.get("events_oom") or 0)
    killed = int(touch.get("events_oom_kill") or 0)
    # The level this watch already calls a session near its limit (--fraction, 0.70: 16.8 GiB of
    # 24 GiB, measured in omp-memory-watch.md), and the anon share a collect-mode box sends the
    # box-health owner at once (--wall-urgent-anon, 0.50). Both are read from the touch so one run
    # has one set of levels.
    warn_fraction = float(touch.get("warn_fraction") or 0.70)
    urgent_anon = float(touch.get("urgent_anon") or 0.50)
    free = reclaimable_bytes(touch)
    if killed:
        return "urgent", (
            f"URGENT, not a forecast: memory.events oom_kill is {killed}, so the kernel has already"
            " killed in this scope."
        ), (
            f"URGENT, and not a forecast: memory.events oom_kill is {killed}. The memcg OOM"
            " killer has ALREADY killed a process in this scope, so reclaim has run out here"
            " before. The next allocation it cannot satisfy takes another process, and an OOM"
            " kill loses every in-flight lane with no exit record."
        )
    if oom:
        return "urgent", (
            f"URGENT: memory.events oom is {oom}: reclaim already failed at memory.max; the next"
            " failure is a kill."
        ), (
            f"URGENT: memory.events oom is {oom}. Reclaim has already failed at memory.max in"
            " this scope and an allocation was about to fail -- the kernel's own condition for"
            " invoking the memcg OOM killer is a usage that reaches the limit and cannot be"
            " reduced. oom_kill is still 0, so nothing has died yet; the next failure is the"
            " kill."
        )
    if free is None:
        return "urgent", (
            "URGENT, conservatively: memory.stat could not be read, so none of the charge can be"
            " shown reclaimable."
        ), (
            "URGENT, conservatively: this scope's memory.stat could not be read, so no part of"
            " its charge can be shown to be page cache. Read it as if none of it is reclaimable."
        )
    if usage >= warn_fraction * limit:
        return "urgent", (
            f"URGENT: {gib(usage)} ({100 * usage / limit:.0f}%) of the ceiling is unreclaimable,"
            " so reclaim is nearly out of material."
        ), (
            f"URGENT: {gib(usage)} of the {gib(limit)} ceiling is unreclaimable"
            f" (anon+shmem+kernel+sock), {100 * usage / limit:.0f}% of it, and only {gib(free)}"
            " is page cache the kernel can drop. That is at or past the"
            f" {100 * warn_fraction:.0f}% level this watch calls a session near its limit, so"
            " reclaim is nearly out of material: when it cannot bring the scope back under"
            " memory.max, the memcg OOM killer runs."
        )
    if anon >= urgent_anon * peak:
        return "urgent", (
            f"URGENT: anon is {gib(anon)}, {100 * anon / peak:.0f}% of the peak, which the kernel"
            " cannot reclaim."
        ), (
            f"URGENT: anonymous memory is {gib(anon)}, {100 * anon / peak:.0f}% of the"
            f" {gib(peak)} peak, and anon is what the kernel cannot hand back. A scope at its"
            " wall holding mostly anon has little left to reclaim, and when reclaim fails the"
            " memcg OOM killer runs."
        )
    return "normal", (
        "NOT URGENT: oom 0 and oom_kill 0, so reclaim brought it back every time; do not kill"
        " builds or halve a shared gate on this."
    ), (
        f"NOT URGENT: {gib(free)} of this scope's {gib(limit)} ceiling --"
        f" {100 * free / limit:.0f}% -- is page cache, which the kernel reclaims on demand, and"
        f" memory.events oom is 0 with oom_kill 0. So every one of the {touch.get('events_max', 0)}"
        " times this scope reached memory.max, direct reclaim brought it down and the allocation"
        " went through: that is exactly what the `max` counter counts. `oom` is the counter that"
        " rises when reclaim fails, and it has not risen here. This scope is NOT one allocation"
        " from a kill; a cache-filled scope can sit on its ceiling for as long as its file"
        " traffic keeps the cache full. Do NOT kill your builds, halve a shared gate, or abandon"
        " a measurement on this report -- that cost is certain, and the kill it would avert is"
        " not coming while these counters hold. Watch oom and oom_kill, not `max`."
    )


def wall_text(touch: dict) -> str:
    """What one scope's owner is told when its scope reaches memory.max.

    No hold, no rate of rise and no forecast: the scope is AT its ceiling now, and every number
    here is read from that one moment.
    """
    limit = touch["limit"]
    peak = touch["peak"]
    session = touch.get("session") or ""
    claim = touch.get("session_claim") or ""
    who = (
        f"session {session}"
        if session
        else (
            f"session UNKNOWN: the id {claim} on its processes is not live on this box and no"
            " live session here matches it"
            if claim
            else "session UNKNOWN (no session id on any command line in the scope, no transcript"
            " open, and the worker directory records none yet)"
        )
    )
    worker = touch.get("worker")
    reasons = []
    if touch.get("events_delta"):
        reasons.append(
            f"the `max` counter of its memory.events rose by {touch['events_delta']} since the"
            f" last poll (now {touch.get('events_max')}): the kernel refused an allocation at"
            " memory.max that many times and had to reclaim to satisfy it"
        )
    if touch.get("peak_touch"):
        reasons.append(
            f"its memory.peak reached {gib(peak)}, {100 * peak / max(limit, 1):.1f}% of the ceiling"
        )
    lines = [
        f"OMP SCOPE REACHED ITS MEMORY WALL: scope {touch['scope']} on {socket.gethostname()}"
        f" ({who}; {identity_phrase(touch)}"
        + (f", ossv-worker {worker}" if worker else ", no ossv-worker name on its command lines") + ")"
        f" is at its {gib(limit)} memory.max{reclaimable_clause(touch)}.",
        "What says so: " + "; and ".join(reasons) + ".",
        f"Numbers now: memory.peak {gib(peak)}, memory.current {gib(touch.get('current') or 0)},"
        f" unreclaimable (anon+shmem+kernel+sock) {gib(touch.get('usage') or 0)}, ceiling"
        f" {gib(limit)} ({touch.get('limit_from')})."
        + swap_sentence(touch.get("swap_max") or "")
        + f" memory.events oom {touch.get('events_oom', 0)}, oom_kill"
        f" {touch.get('events_oom_kill', 0)}.",
        f"Where that memory is, by memory.stat: {stat_split(touch.get('stats') or {})}.",
        pressure_line(touch.get("cgroup") or ""),
        f"The scope holds {touch.get('task_count') or 0} processes holding"
        f" {gib(touch.get('rss_sum') or 0)} of RSS between them,"
        f" {100 * int(touch.get('rss_sum') or 0) / max(peak, 1):.0f}% of the peak."
        " The three largest by RSS:",
    ]
    for pid, rss, command in touch.get("top") or []:
        lines.append(f"  {gib(rss)}  pid {pid}  {command[:200] or '(no command line)'}")
    if not touch.get("top"):
        lines.append("  (none readable: every process in the scope ended before it could be read)")
    lines.append(
        "Why you are hearing this and did not before: the crossing alert needs the scope's"
        " UNRECLAIMABLE memory to sit above 70% of the ceiling for 60 s. A scope whose pressure"
        " is page cache, or one that reaches the ceiling and falls back inside one 30 s period,"
        " never meets that and still spends its whole budget. On ossv-agent-open on 2026-10-30,"
        " 31 of 95 worker scopes had touched their 24 GiB wall and 4 were reported."
    )
    state, verdict = wall_state(touch)
    lines.append(
        "DO, first and whatever the cause: write your handover files now -- what each lane was"
        " doing and where its work is. That costs seconds, and it is right whether this scope is"
        " in danger or not."
    )
    lines.append(f"What the counters say about this scope ({state}): {verdict}")
    lines.append("If you do shed, shed the right thing: " + wall_advice(touch))
    lines.append(
        f"This watch only detects; it kills nothing. One report per scope per"
        f" {human(touch.get('cooldown') or 900)}. From omp-memory-watch"
        f" ({SELF_PATH}) on {socket.gethostname()}, a systemd --user"
        " timer every 30 s."
    )
    return "\n".join(lines)


def wall_head(touch: dict) -> str:
    """One touch in one paragraph: where, how full, the verdict, and who acts.

    To the scope's own session; or, when no live session on this box owns the scope, to the
    box-health owner, since nobody else would hear it.
    """
    state, short, _verdict = wall_verdict(touch)
    owner = touch.get("session") or ""
    who = f"session {owner}, {identity_phrase(touch)}" if owner else f"no live session on this box owns it; {identity_phrase(touch)}"
    worker = f", ossv-worker {touch['worker']}" if touch.get("worker") else ""
    act = (
        "DO: have that lane write its handover and shed what the report names, or stop it."
        if touch.get("lane_of")
        else "DO: write your handover files now; shed only what the report names."
        if owner
        else "So the box-health owner hears it: find who runs this scope (its processes are in the report)."
    )
    return (
        (lane_note(touch) + " " if touch.get("lane_of") else "")
        + f"OMP SCOPE AT ITS MEMORY WALL ({'URGENT' if state == 'urgent' else 'not urgent'}): scope"
        f" {touch['scope']} on {socket.gethostname()} ({who}{worker}) reached {gib(touch.get('peak') or 0)}"
        f" of its {gib(touch.get('limit') or 0)} memory.max{reclaimable_clause(touch)}. {short} {act}"
    )


def wall_burst_head(recent: list[dict], window: float) -> str:
    """The box-health owner's one message for several scopes at their wall in one window."""
    scopes = len({touch.get("scope") for touch in recent})
    urgent = sum(1 for touch in recent if touch.get("urgent"))
    orphans = sum(1 for touch in recent if not touch.get("session"))
    count = f"{scopes} scopes" + (f" ({len(recent)} touches)" if len(recent) != scopes else "")
    return (
        f"MEMORY WALL BURST on {socket.gethostname()}: {count} reached their memory.max in the last"
        f" {human(window)}; {urgent} urgent by their own counters"
        + (f", {orphans} with no live owning session" if orphans else "")
        + ". Each owning session has its own report. Look for one cause behind them: a build or gate"
        " shared across lanes, or the box itself short of memory."
    )


def wall_burst_report(recent: list[dict], window: float, level: int) -> str:
    """The burst's report: one row a touch, then each touch's own report file."""
    lines = [
        wall_burst_head(recent, window),
        "",
        "Columns: time, box, worker, peak of ceiling, page cache, anonymous, session.",
    ]
    ordered = sorted(recent, key=lambda touch: touch.get("at") or 0)
    lines.extend(digest_row(touch) for touch in ordered)
    lines.append("")
    lines.append("Each touch's whole report (the memory.stat split, the processes, the verdict, the advice):")
    lines.extend(f"  {touch.get('scope')}: {touch.get('report') or 'none written'}" for touch in ordered)
    lines.append(
        f"This box-health message is sent when {level} or more touches on this box reach their"
        " wall in one window, and again only when that count doubles; a single touch goes to its own"
        " session, or here when no live session owns the scope. From omp-memory-watch"
        f" ({SELF_PATH}) on {socket.gethostname()}."
    )
    return "\n".join(lines)


def urgent_reason(touch: dict, options: argparse.Namespace) -> str:
    """Why this touch cannot wait for the fleet collector's hourly digest, or "" (collect mode).

    Two shapes end in a kill rather than in reclaim. Anonymous memory is the memory the kernel
    cannot give back, so a scope at its wall holding mostly anon has nothing left to reclaim:
    the next allocation is the OOM killer's. And an oom_kill that has already happened is not a
    forecast at all. Everything else -- a scope at its wall on page cache, which the kernel
    drops and carries on -- waits for the collector.
    """
    if int((touch.get("stats") or {}).get("anon", 0)) >= options.wall_urgent_anon * max(int(touch.get("peak") or 0), 1):
        return (
            f"anon {gib((touch.get('stats') or {}).get('anon', 0))} is"
            f" {100 * int((touch.get('stats') or {}).get('anon', 0)) / max(int(touch.get('peak') or 1), 1):.0f}%"
            f" of the {gib(touch.get('peak') or 0)} peak, which the kernel cannot reclaim"
        )
    if int(touch.get("events_oom_kill") or 0):
        return f"memory.events oom_kill is {touch['events_oom_kill']}: the kernel has already killed in this scope"
    return ""


def urgent_text(touch: dict, reason: str) -> str:
    """The head of a collect-mode touch sent the moment it is seen, to the box-health owner."""
    worker = touch.get("worker") or "no worker name"
    session = touch.get("session") or "session unknown"
    return (
        f"SCOPE AT ITS MEMORY WALL, NOT WAITING FOR THE DIGEST, on {socket.gethostname()}:"
        f" {touch.get('scope')} ({worker}, {session}) reached {gib(touch.get('peak') or 0)} of"
        f" {gib(touch.get('limit') or 0)}. Why this one is immediate: {reason}. Its owner has"
        " the same report."
    )


def digest_row(touch: dict) -> str:
    """One scope on one line: box, worker, peak, and the file/anon split."""
    stats = touch.get("stats") or {}
    peak = max(int(touch.get("peak") or 0), 1)
    return (
        f"  {iso(touch.get('at'))}  {touch.get('box') or socket.gethostname().split('.')[0]}"
        f"  {touch.get('worker') or 'no worker name'}"
        f"  peak {gib(touch.get('peak') or 0)} of {gib(touch.get('limit') or 0)}"
        f"  file {gib(stats.get('file', 0))} ({100 * int(stats.get('file', 0)) / peak:.0f}%)"
        f"  anon {gib(stats.get('anon', 0))} ({100 * int(stats.get('anon', 0)) / peak:.0f}%)"
        + (f"  oom_kill {touch['events_oom_kill']}" if int(touch.get("events_oom_kill") or 0) else "")
        + f"  {touch.get('session') or 'session unknown'}"
    )


def wall_digest_text(pending: list[dict], window: float, scope_of_text: str = "") -> str:
    """The fleet collector's batched view: one line per scope that reached its wall.

    Only collect mode feeds it: oss-verifier's wall-digest-collect.sh drains each lane VM with
    --wall-pending-json and renders this, one message an hour for the fleet (Sami, 2026-10-31:
    a per-box digest every 15 minutes was eight messages an hour saying the same thing). Each
    scope's own owner already has the whole report, and the two shapes that end in a kill --
    anon above half the peak, and an oom_kill that already happened -- do not wait for it.
    """
    where = scope_of_text or socket.gethostname()
    boxes = sorted({str(touch.get("box") or socket.gethostname().split(".")[0]) for touch in pending})
    lines = [
        f"SCOPES AT THEIR MEMORY WALL ({where}): {len(pending)} touch(es) on"
        f" {len(boxes)} box(es) in the last {human(window)} -- {', '.join(boxes)}. Each is a"
        " scope whose memory.events `max` counter rose or whose memory.peak reached its"
        " ceiling, with no hold, so these are the touches the 70%-for-60 s crossing alert"
        " cannot see. Columns: time, box, worker, peak, page cache, anonymous, session.",
    ]
    lines.extend(digest_row(touch) for touch in sorted(pending, key=lambda t: t.get("at") or 0))
    lines.append(
        "Anything here is reclaimable pressure: a scope whose anon passed half its peak, or one"
        " the kernel has already killed in, was sent the moment it was seen and is not in this"
        " list for the first time. Each scope's own owner has the whole report. From"
        " omp-memory-watch."
    )
    return "\n".join(lines)


def wall_touch(
    target: dict, walls: dict, options: argparse.Namespace, now: float, cold_start: bool
) -> dict | None:
    """Decide whether this capped scope has REACHED its wall since the last poll, and report it.

    Returns the touch to report, or None. The wall memory is keyed by scope and never by pid:
    one scope holds several `omp` processes (the session, its js-eval helper), the crossing
    logic follows whichever is largest, and a flip between them must not look like a new scope
    and report the same backlog again.
    """
    key = target["key"]
    limit = target["limit"]
    events = target.get("events") or {}
    events_max = int(events.get("max", 0))
    peak = int(target.get("peak_bytes") or 0)
    memory = walls.setdefault(key, {})
    known = "events_max" in memory
    previous_max = int(memory.get("events_max", 0))
    previous_peak = int(memory.get("peak", 0))
    memory["events_max"] = events_max
    memory["peak"] = max(previous_peak, peak)
    if cold_start and not known:
        # Seeded, not reported: see run_once. A counter already above zero dates from before
        # this state file existed.
        return None
    delta = events_max - previous_max if known else events_max
    peak_floor = limit * options.wall_fraction
    peak_touch = peak >= peak_floor and peak > previous_peak
    if delta <= 0 and not peak_touch:
        return None
    # A capped job working as designed: the kernel refused no allocation it could not satisfy
    # (oom 0), killed nothing (oom_kill 0), and at least half the ceiling is page cache it can
    # drop. Jailed `omp -p` readers on an 8 GiB run-r*.scope sat on their wall that way with
    # 76-94% of it reclaimable and were reported every few minutes to the box-health owner
    # (2026-11-04, e.g. pid 1047986 at 06:29Z): a notice nobody could act on. The touch is
    # counted, not dropped, so the next real report says how many quiet ones came before it,
    # and it does not start the cooldown, so a touch that turns dangerous is told at once.
    oom = int(events.get("oom", 0))
    oom_kill = int(events.get("oom_kill", 0))
    free = reclaimable_bytes({"stats": target.get("stats"), "current": target.get("current"),
                              "usage": target.get("usage")})
    if oom == 0 and oom_kill == 0 and free is not None and free >= limit * options.wall_quiet_reclaimable:
        memory["quiet"] = int(memory.get("quiet", 0)) + 1
        return None
    last = memory.get("reported_at")
    if last and now - last < options.wall_cooldown:
        # Inside the cooldown the touch is real and already reported; count it so the next
        # report can say how many times it happened rather than dropping the fact.
        memory["suppressed"] = int(memory.get("suppressed", 0)) + 1
        return None
    pids = scope_tasks(target["cgroup"])
    worker, directory = worker_of(pids)
    from_worker = worker_session(directory)
    named, omp_pid = scope_session(pids, target["pid"])
    session = from_worker or named
    memory["reported_at"] = now
    suppressed = int(memory.pop("suppressed", 0))
    quiet = int(memory.pop("quiet", 0))
    # Every process in the scope, not only the three named: the three largest explained 10-29%
    # of a scope at its ceiling in the open4 and open6 digests of 2026-10-30, and the question
    # that leaves -- one runaway, a crowd of small processes, or memory no process holds -- is
    # answered by the whole RSS sum against the memory.stat split, not by a longer top list.
    by_rss = [(pid, rss_of(pid), cmdline_of(pid)) for pid in pids]
    by_rss = [row for row in by_rss if row[1] > 0]
    by_rss.sort(key=lambda row: row[1], reverse=True)
    return dict(
        at=now,
        scope=target["scope"],
        cgroup=target["cgroup"],
        key=key,
        session=session,
        worker_session=from_worker,
        **omp_identity(omp_pid),
        lane_of="",
        session_note="",
        session_claim="",
        worker=worker,
        limit=limit,
        limit_from=target["limit_from"],
        peak=peak,
        current=target["current"],
        usage=target["usage"],
        swap_max=target.get("swap_max") or "",
        events_max=events_max,
        events_delta=delta,
        events_oom=int(events.get("oom", 0)),
        events_oom_kill=int(events.get("oom_kill", 0)),
        peak_touch=peak_touch,
        task_count=len(pids),
        rss_sum=sum(rss for _pid, rss, _command in by_rss),
        stats={name: int(value) for name, value in (target.get("stats") or {}).items()},
        top=by_rss[: options.wall_top],
        suppressed=suppressed,
        quiet=quiet,
        cooldown=options.wall_cooldown,
        # The levels `wall_state` judges this touch against, carried with it so the report and
        # the run that produced it can never disagree about what "near the limit" means.
        warn_fraction=options.fraction,
        urgent_anon=options.wall_urgent_anon,
    )


def clear_text(scope: str, incident: dict, cause: str, now: float) -> str:
    opened = incident.get("opened_at") or now
    session = incident.get("session") or "unknown"
    return (
        f"OMP SESSION MEMORY CLEARED: session {session} (omp pid {incident.get('pid')}, scope {scope})"
        f" is back under {gib(incident.get('warn_at') or 0)}. What cleared it: {cause}. "
        f"The crossing opened at {iso(opened)}, cleared at {iso(now)}, lasted {human(now - opened)},"
        f" and peaked at {gib(incident.get('peak') or 0)} of a {gib(incident.get('limit') or 0)} ceiling. "
        "No action is needed. From omp-memory-watch on " + socket.gethostname() + ".\n"
        + pressure_line(incident.get("pressure_cgroup") or incident.get("cgroup") or "")
    )


def clear_head(scope: str, incident: dict, cause: str) -> str:
    """A clear in one line: who, what level, and why it cleared."""
    return (
        f"OMP SESSION MEMORY CLEARED: session {incident.get('session') or 'unknown'}, scope"
        f" {incident.get('scope') or scope}, is back under {gib(incident.get('warn_at') or 0)}:"
        f" {cause}. No action is needed."
    )


# A machine whose box health belongs to one session, not to this box's role holder, sets
# BOX_HEALTH_OWNER to that session id: the OSS Verify lane VMs run these watches from their own
# dotfiles and their alerts go to the fleet monitoring owner.
OWNER_RE = re.compile(r"01[0-9a-f]{6}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{12}\Z")


def box_health_owner() -> str:
    """The session id BOX_HEALTH_OWNER names, or "" for the role-then-SRE default.

    An unusable value is refused at start, not at the first crossing: a watch that drops its
    alerts quietly is worse than one that does not run.
    """
    owner = (os.environ.get("BOX_HEALTH_OWNER") or "").strip()
    if owner and not OWNER_RE.fullmatch(owner):
        raise SystemExit(
            f"BOX_HEALTH_OWNER={owner!r} is not an omp session id"
            " (01xxxxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx); unset it for the box-health role"
        )
    return owner


def tell_box_health(envoy: Envoy, text: str) -> str:
    """Tell this box's health owner, and say who heard it.

    With BOX_HEALTH_OWNER set, that session is the only recipient and nothing is published to
    the role. Otherwise the role is the address while somebody holds it; an unheld role answers
    non-200, and then the SRE session hears it instead.
    """
    owner = box_health_owner()
    if owner:
        return owner if envoy.send(owner, text) else ""
    if envoy.publish(BOX_HEALTH_ROLE_TOPIC, text):
        return BOX_HEALTH_ROLE_TOPIC
    if envoy.send(SRE, text):
        return SRE
    return ""


def tell(envoy: Envoy, target: str, text: str) -> bool:
    """Send to a session id, or publish to the box-health role when that is the recipient."""
    if target == BOX_HEALTH_ROLE_TOPIC:
        return bool(tell_box_health(envoy, text))
    return envoy.send(target, text)


def send_clear(envoy: Envoy, options: argparse.Namespace, scope: str, incident: dict, cause: str, now: float) -> bool:
    """Tell the session that heard the alert that its crossing cleared, in one line.

    Never the box-health owner: a clear asks nothing of it. In the 48 h to 2026-11-03 it got 19
    clears, every one saying "No action is needed", 14 of them within 4 min of their alert.
    """
    if not incident.get("alerted_at") or incident.get("cleared_at"):
        return False
    incident["cleared_at"] = now
    # A lane's alert went to its parent (route_lane); so does its clear.
    session = incident.get("lane_of") or incident.get("session") or ""
    if not session or session not in (incident.get("told") or [session]):
        log(f"{scope}: CLEARED ({cause}); nobody told: no session heard the alert")
        return True
    report = write_report(options, "cleared", clear_text(scope, incident, cause, now), now)
    text = short_message(clear_head(scope, incident, cause), report)
    if not options.dry_run:
        envoy.send(session, text)
    log(f"{scope}: CLEARED ({cause}); told session {session}")
    if options.print_alert:
        print(text)
    return True


def oom_copy(
    envoy: Envoy, options: argparse.Namespace, state, key: str, incident: dict, oom_kill: int, now: float
) -> bool:
    """Copy the box-health owner, once a crossing, when the kernel OOM-kills in a scope whose
    session was told it was near its limit: the forecast came true, and the session may be gone.

    `oom_kill` is the scope's memory.events counter now; the crossing keeps the value it had when
    it opened. A crossing opened by a version of this watch that kept none starts its count here.
    """
    base = incident.get("oom_kill_base")
    if base is None:
        incident["oom_kill_base"] = oom_kill
        return False
    if oom_kill <= int(base) or incident.get("oom_told_at"):
        return False
    incident["oom_told_at"] = now
    head = oom_head(key, incident, int(base), oom_kill)
    body = (
        f"{head}\n\nThe alert its session was sent: {incident.get('report') or '(no report recorded)'}"
        f"\n\nThe crossing as this run reads it:\n{alert_text(key, incident, now, 'at the OOM kill')}"
    )
    text = short_message(head, write_report(options, "oom-kill", body, now))
    state.commit(options)
    heard = "(dry run)" if options.dry_run else tell_box_health(envoy, text)
    log(f"{key}: OOM KILL during the crossing (oom_kill {base} -> {oom_kill}) -> {heard or 'nobody: every route refused it'}")
    if options.print_alert:
        print(text)
    return True


# ---------------------------------------------------- prefer the runaway child over the session

# The procfs this pass reads and writes. A module constant so the tests can point it at a tree.
PROC_ROOT = Path("/proc")


def parent_of(pid: int) -> int:
    """Field 4 of /proc/<pid>/stat, or 0 when the process is gone."""
    try:
        data = (PROC_ROOT / str(pid) / "stat").read_text()
        return int(data[data.rfind(")") + 2 :].split()[1])
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
        return 0


def oom_bias_pass(
    omps: list[tuple[int, str]], adj: int, dry_run: bool, deadline: float = float("inf")
) -> dict[str, int]:
    """Raise every process in a capped omp scope, except the session's own omp, to `adj`.

    When a scope reaches its MemoryMax the memcg OOM killer kills the member with the largest
    badness: its RSS + swap + page tables, plus oom_score_adj * (the scope's limit) / 1000. With
    every adj at 0 that is the session's omp, which held 14.9 of the 24 GiB at the 2026-10-27
    kill (dispatch://INFRA-602), and about 40 subagents died with it. An unprivileged process may
    raise an oom_score_adj of its own uid but never lower one, so omp cannot be biased down;
    every other member is biased up instead. At +500 under a 24 GiB limit each such process
    scores 12 GiB more: a 3.2 GiB tsgo (15.2) is killed before a 1.7 GiB omp, and only a member
    smaller than the omp by more than 12 GiB of RSS loses to it.

    The rule protects the SESSION's omp, not every process named omp: the one omp in the scope
    whose parent is outside the scope -- the process `shims/omp` starts through `systemd-run
    --scope`, and which owns the session. A nested omp (a subagent, a js-eval helper) has its
    parent inside the scope and is biased like any other child: a runaway subagent holding
    10 GiB is a far better victim than the session and all its other lanes. A scope where no omp
    has its parent outside is left with every omp unbiased, which is how it was before this pass.

    Only scopes with a limit of their OWN are touched. An uncapped session shares its login
    scope with every other session there, and that scope is not this session's to bias.

    It never lowers an adj (a member already at or above `adj` is counted and left), and never
    writes a process it may not: EPERM (another uid, as in an agent box) is counted as refused.

    `omps` is every omp process on the machine (`omp_processes`), so a member's name is not read
    again: each /proc open costs about 1 ms at load 400, and a scope can hold hundreds.
    """
    counts = {"scopes": 0, "raised": 0, "already": 0, "refused": 0, "gone": 0, "failed": 0, "no_leader": 0}
    every_omp = {pid for pid, _cgroup in omps}
    cgroups = sorted({cgroup for _pid, cgroup in omps})
    for cgroup in cgroups:
        if time.monotonic() > deadline:
            counts["unfinished"] = counts.get("unfinished", 0) + 1
            continue
        if not effective_limit(cgroup)[2]:
            continue
        counts["scopes"] += 1
        members = scope_tasks(cgroup)
        inside = set(members)
        omp_members = [pid for pid in members if pid in every_omp]
        protected = {pid for pid in omp_members if parent_of(pid) not in inside}
        if not protected:
            counts["no_leader"] += 1
            protected = set(omp_members)
        for pid in members:
            if pid in protected:
                continue
            path = PROC_ROOT / str(pid) / "oom_score_adj"
            try:
                current = int(path.read_text().strip())
            except (FileNotFoundError, ProcessLookupError):
                counts["gone"] += 1
                continue
            except (OSError, ValueError):
                counts["failed"] += 1
                continue
            if current >= adj:
                counts["already"] += 1
                continue
            if dry_run:
                counts["raised"] += 1
                continue
            try:
                path.write_text(f"{adj}\n")
                counts["raised"] += 1
            except PermissionError:
                counts["refused"] += 1
            except (FileNotFoundError, ProcessLookupError):
                counts["gone"] += 1
            except OSError:
                counts["failed"] += 1
    return counts


def oom_bias_note(counts: dict[str, int], adj: int, dry_run: bool) -> str:
    verb = "would raise" if dry_run else "raised"
    note = (
        f"oom bias +{adj}: {counts['scopes']} capped scope(s), {verb} {counts['raised']},"
        f" {counts['already']} already at or above, {counts['refused']} refused (EPERM),"
        f" {counts['gone']} gone"
    )
    if counts["failed"]:
        note += f", {counts['failed']} failed"
    if counts["no_leader"]:
        note += f", {counts['no_leader']} scope(s) with no session omp found (every omp left)"
    if counts.get("unfinished"):
        note += f", {counts['unfinished']} scope(s) left for the next run (out of time)"
    return note


# ------------------------------------------- give a session that has no scope of its own one
#
# shims/omp starts each session in a `run-*.scope` with a memory ceiling and OOMPolicy=continue.
# When both of its scope probes fail -- two 25 s user-manager timeouts 10 s apart at load 450 --
# it starts the session in whatever cgroup its shell is in: the login scope (session-c5.scope,
# hundreds of other processes) or tmux's. Session 01a19679 started that way at 11:57Z on
# 2026-11-02 and ran with no ceiling until it was moved by hand at 13:36-13:45Z. Sessions started
# before shims/omp c31519b run in a scope with systemd's default OOMPolicy=stop, which ends the
# whole session when the kernel kills one runaway child (2026-11-02T03:22:08Z, 40 subagents).
# This pass moves both into a new delegated `run-c<16 hex of the session>.scope` with
# OOMPolicy=continue. No process is signalled, and the memory each one already holds stays
# charged where it is: cgroup v2 moves a process, not its pages.
CEILING_FLOOR = 24 << 30  # shims/omp's smallest default, dispatch://INFRA-898


def default_ceiling() -> int:
    """shims/omp's default ceiling: the larger of 24 GiB and MemTotal/20, in whole GiB
    (dispatch://INFRA-993). 24 GiB on sami-rocqperf (251 GiB), 109 GiB on sami-devbox (2.1 TiB)."""
    return max(CEILING_FLOOR, (mem_total() // 20) >> 30 << 30)


def ceiling_cap() -> int:
    """shims/omp's clamp for one session: four times its default."""
    return 4 * default_ceiling()


# When the pass must stop, in seconds from the run's start; a systemd call is begun only with
# BUS_TIMEOUT left before it.
ADOPT_DEADLINE = 40.0
BUS_TIMEOUT = 10.0
ADOPT_MIN_AGE = 60.0
RUN_SCOPE_RE = re.compile(r"run-[^/]+\.scope")
# The cgroups a session lands in when the shim falls back: the login session's, or tmux's.
ADOPTABLE_RE = re.compile(r"(session-[^/]+|tmux-spawn-[^/]+)\.scope")
BUS = ["busctl", "--user", "call", "org.freedesktop.systemd1", "/org/freedesktop/systemd1",
       "org.freedesktop.systemd1.Manager"]


def systemd_user(argv: list[str], timeout: float = BUS_TIMEOUT) -> tuple[bool, str]:
    """Run one systemctl or busctl call: (it succeeded, its stdout or its error). Tests replace it."""
    try:
        done = subprocess.run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as error:
        return False, str(error)
    return done.returncode == 0, (done.stdout if done.returncode == 0 else done.stderr).strip()


def proc_words(pid: int, name: str) -> list[str]:
    """/proc/<pid>/cmdline or environ as its NUL-separated words, [] when unreadable."""
    try:
        return [w for w in (PROC_ROOT / str(pid) / name).read_bytes().decode("utf8", "replace").split("\0") if w]
    except OSError:
        return []


def ceiling_bytes(text: str) -> int | None:
    """`24G`, `512M`, a byte count -> bytes, clamped at ceiling_cap(); None when it is not one."""
    match = re.fullmatch(r"([0-9]+)([KMGT]?)", (text or "").strip())
    if not match:
        return None
    value = int(match.group(1)) << {"": 0, "K": 10, "M": 20, "G": 30, "T": 40}[match.group(2)]
    return min(value, ceiling_cap()) if value > 0 else None


def adopt_session_id(pid: int) -> str:
    """The session an omp runs: the id in the last path component of its --resume (a nested
    transcript path names its parent first), else the OMP_SESSION_ID it inherited, else ""."""
    session = resume_session(proc_words(pid, "cmdline"))
    if session:
        return session
    env = dict(word.split("=", 1) for word in proc_words(pid, "environ") if "=" in word)
    return env.get("OMP_SESSION_ID", "") if UUID_RE.fullmatch(env.get("OMP_SESSION_ID", "")) else ""


def children_by_parent() -> dict[int, list[int]]:
    children: dict[int, list[int]] = {}
    for entry in os.listdir(PROC_ROOT):
        if entry.isdigit():
            children.setdefault(parent_of(int(entry)), []).append(int(entry))
    return children


def tree_in(pid: int, cgroup: str, children: dict[int, list[int]]) -> list[int]:
    """The process and its descendants that are still in `cgroup`, the process first. A child
    that already has a cgroup of its own (a tool's own scope, a box) is not the session's to move."""
    found, stack = [], [pid]
    while stack:
        current = stack.pop()
        if cgroup_of(current) == cgroup:
            found.append(current)
        stack.extend(children.get(current, []))
    return found


def scope_policies(units: list[str]) -> dict[str, str]:
    """OOMPolicy of each unit, from one `systemctl show` call."""
    ok, out = systemd_user(["systemctl", "--user", "show", "-p", "Id", "-p", "OOMPolicy", *units])
    found: dict[str, str] = {}
    if ok:
        for block in out.split("\n\n"):
            fields = dict(line.split("=", 1) for line in block.splitlines() if "=" in line)
            if fields.get("Id") and fields.get("OOMPolicy"):
                found[fields["Id"]] = fields["OOMPolicy"]
    return found


def attach(unit: str, pids: list[int], deadline: float) -> tuple[int, int, int]:
    """(attached, vanished or refused, left for the next run). One pid per call: a pid that has
    exited fails the whole call."""
    attached = failed = 0
    for index, pid in enumerate(pids):
        if time.monotonic() > deadline - BUS_TIMEOUT:
            return attached, failed, len(pids) - index
        ok, _ = systemd_user(BUS + ["AttachProcessesToUnit", "ssau", unit, "/", "1", str(pid)])
        attached, failed = attached + ok, failed + (not ok)
    return attached, failed, 0


def scope_adopt_pass(
    omps: list[tuple[int, str]], state, options: argparse.Namespace, now: float,
    deadline: float = float("inf"),
) -> str:
    """Move each top-level host omp session that has no `run-*.scope` of its own, or whose
    `run-*.scope` has OOMPolicy=stop, into a new one; log each move; say what it did."""
    dry_run = options.dry_run
    adopted: dict = state.data.setdefault("adopted", {})
    policies: dict = state.data.setdefault("scope_policy", {})
    uid = os.getuid()
    user = f"/user.slice/user-{uid}.slice/"
    manager = f"{user}user@{uid}.service/"
    every_omp = {pid for pid, _ in omps}
    notes: list[str] = []
    children: dict[int, list[int]] | None = None

    # The rest of an earlier move: the session's own processes still in the cgroup it left.
    for unit, record in list(adopted.items()):
        pid = int(record.get("pid") or 0)
        if pid_start(pid) != record.get("start") or not cgroup_of(pid).endswith("/" + unit):
            adopted.pop(unit)
            continue
        if record.get("kind") == "stop":
            rest = scope_tasks(record["from"])
        else:
            children = children if children is not None else children_by_parent()
            rest = tree_in(pid, record["from"], children)
        if rest and not dry_run:
            done, failed, left = attach(unit, rest, deadline)
            notes.append(f"{unit}: attached {done} more, {failed} gone, {left} left")

    # Top-level host sessions: this user's, not a subagent of another omp, and up for a minute,
    # so an `omp` a script runs for a moment is not given a scope of its own.
    ticks = os.sysconf("SC_CLK_TCK")
    try:
        uptime = float((PROC_ROOT / "uptime").read_text().split()[0])
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
        uptime = 0.0
    # omp's own workers (`omp __omp_worker_<name>`, such as the worker broker) are not sessions:
    # their parent is init, but they name no session. A broker gets a scope of its own from
    # broker_scope_pass instead.
    sessions = [
        (pid, cgroup) for pid, cgroup in sorted(omps)
        if cgroup.startswith(user) and parent_of(pid) not in every_omp
        and uptime - pid_start(pid) / ticks >= ADOPT_MIN_AGE
        and not is_omp_worker(pid)
    ]
    live_run_scopes = {
        cgroup.rpartition("/")[2] for _, cgroup in sessions
        if cgroup.startswith(manager) and RUN_SCOPE_RE.fullmatch(cgroup.rpartition("/")[2])
    }
    # A scope's policy never changes and its name is never reused: read once, kept while it lives.
    unknown = sorted(live_run_scopes - set(policies))
    if unknown and time.monotonic() < deadline - BUS_TIMEOUT:
        policies.update(scope_policies(unknown))
    for leaf in set(policies) - live_run_scopes:
        policies.pop(leaf)
    candidates: list[tuple[int, str, str]] = []
    taken: set[str] = set()
    for pid, cgroup in sessions:
        leaf = cgroup.rpartition("/")[2]
        if leaf in live_run_scopes and policies.get(leaf) == "stop" and cgroup not in taken:
            taken.add(cgroup)  # one move per scope: it takes every process in it
            candidates.append((pid, cgroup, "stop"))
        elif not RUN_SCOPE_RE.fullmatch(leaf) and ADOPTABLE_RE.fullmatch(leaf):
            candidates.append((pid, cgroup, "unscoped"))

    moved = 0
    for pid, cgroup, kind in candidates:
        if time.monotonic() > deadline - BUS_TIMEOUT:
            notes.append(f"{len(candidates) - moved} session(s) left for the next run (out of time)")
            break
        sid = adopt_session_id(pid)
        old = cgroup.rpartition("/")[2]
        if kind == "stop":
            ok, out = systemd_user(["systemctl", "--user", "show", "-p", "MemoryMax", "-p", "MemorySwapMax", "-p", "TasksMax", old])
            props = dict(line.split("=", 1) for line in out.splitlines() if "=" in line) if ok else {}
            if not props:
                notes.append(f"{old}: its limits could not be read ({out}); left")
                continue
            limits = [(key, props[key]) for key in ("MemoryMax", "MemorySwapMax", "TasksMax")
                      if props.get(key) not in (None, "", "infinity")]
            members = scope_tasks(cgroup)
        else:
            env = dict(word.split("=", 1) for word in proc_words(pid, "environ") if "=" in word)
            # shims/omp exports the ceiling it asked for before it falls back, so a session keeps
            # its own value; anything else gets the shim's default.
            ceiling = ceiling_bytes(env.get("OMP_MEMORY_SCOPE", "")) or default_ceiling()
            children = children if children is not None else children_by_parent()
            members = tree_in(pid, cgroup, children)
            rss = sum(rss_of(member) for member in members)
            if rss >= ceiling:
                log(f"scope adopt: left omp {pid} ({sid or 'session unknown'}) in {old}: its processes"
                    f" hold {gib(rss)}, at or above the {gib(ceiling)} ceiling it would get")
                continue
            limits = [("MemoryMax", str(ceiling)), ("MemorySwapMax", "0")]
        base = "run-c" + (sid.replace("-", "")[:16] if sid else f"pid{pid}")
        unit, number = f"{base}.scope", 2
        while (CGROUP_ROOT / manager.strip("/") / "app.slice" / unit).exists():
            unit, number = f"{base}-{number}.scope", number + 1
        what = (f"omp {pid} ({sid or 'session unknown'}, {len(members)} process(es)) from {old} into"
                f" {unit}, OOMPolicy=continue, " + ", ".join(f"{k}={v}" for k, v in limits))
        if dry_run:
            log(f"scope adopt: would move {what}")
            moved += 1
            continue
        props = ["Description", "s", f"omp session {sid or pid} (moved from {old} by omp-memory-watch)",
                 "Slice", "s", "app.slice", "Delegate", "b", "true", "OOMPolicy", "s", "continue"]
        for key, value in limits:
            props += [key, "t", value]
        ok, out = systemd_user(BUS + ["StartTransientUnit", "ssa(sv)a(sa(sv))", unit, "fail",
                                      str(len(props) // 3 + 1), *props, "PIDs", "au", "1", str(pid), "0"])
        if not ok:
            # An omp that exited between the listing and this call makes systemd answer "No such
            # process": nothing is left to move and nothing to retry (15 such FAILED lines in
            # 6 h on sami-rocqperf, 2026-11-03). A refusal for an omp still running is a failure.
            if (PROC_ROOT / str(pid)).exists():
                log(f"scope adopt: FAILED to create {unit} for omp {pid} in {old}: {out}")
                notes.append(f"{old}: create failed")
            continue
        adopted[unit] = {"pid": pid, "start": pid_start(pid), "from": cgroup, "kind": kind, "sid": sid, "at": now}
        state.commit(options)
        done, failed, left = attach(unit, [member for member in members if member != pid], deadline)
        moved += 1
        log(f"scope adopt: moved {what}; attached {done + 1}, {failed} gone, {left} left for the next run")
    verb = "would move" if dry_run else "moved"
    return f"scopes: {verb} {moved} session(s)" + (f" ({'; '.join(notes)})" if notes else "")

# ------------------------------------- give each worker broker, and each lane's services, a scope
#
# omp starts one worker broker per project directory (`omp __omp_worker_daemon_broker`,
# packages/coding-agent/src/launch/client.ts #spawnBroker) as a plain detached child of the first
# session that needs one. A child keeps its parent's cgroup, so the broker, and every background
# service any session starts through it (the bash tool's services, proc://), ran in that first
# session's scope: on 2026-11-03 the broker of one project checkout (pid 77103) and the
# services of 14 sessions sat in the chief's run-c01a13dcae9797264.scope, under the chief's 24 GiB
# ceiling, with earlyoom's kill notices sent to the chief. That scope stays while any process is in
# it, after its session has gone, and stopping it kills them all.
#
# This pass moves each broker into `omp-broker-<16 hex of its runtime dir>.scope`, and each service
# the broker's `daemons/*/meta.json` records with an owner session (and the service's process
# tree) into `run-c<16 hex of the owner>-svc.scope`, one per owner, described exactly
# `omp session <owner id>`: the names earlyoom-kill-watch reads. A process the broker runs for no
# session (the shared headless browser) stays with the broker. Both get shims/omp's ceiling and
# OOMPolicy=continue. Only processes still in a cgroup the broker's tree inherited are moved: one
# a service put in a scope of its own is left. No process is signalled, and memory already held
# stays charged where it is (cgroup v2 moves a process, not its pages).
BROKER_ARG = "__omp_worker_daemon_broker"
OMP_HOME = str(Path.home() / ".omp") + "/"
# When the pass must stop, in seconds from the run's start; it runs after the adoption pass.
BROKER_DEADLINE = 70.0
LIVE_DAEMON_STATES = ("starting", "running", "ready")


def broker_unit(runtime_dir: str) -> str:
    return "omp-broker-" + hashlib.sha256(runtime_dir.encode()).hexdigest()[:16] + ".scope"


def service_unit(owner: str) -> str:
    return "run-c" + owner.replace("-", "")[:16] + "-svc.scope"


def broker_daemons(runtime_dir: str, unknown: set[int], deadline: float) -> tuple[dict[int, str], bool]:
    """({pid: owner} for the live services of a broker whose pid is in `unknown`, whether every
    record was read or every pid found). Newest meta.json first, stopping once every pid is
    found: a broker's directory keeps every service it ever ran (1,015 for one broker on
    2026-11-03), and a new service's record is the newest."""
    found: dict[int, str] = {}
    try:
        metas = sorted(Path(runtime_dir, "daemons").glob("*/meta.json"),
                       key=lambda path: path.stat().st_mtime, reverse=True)
    except OSError:
        return found, False
    for meta in metas:
        if not unknown - found.keys():
            return found, True
        if time.monotonic() > deadline - BUS_TIMEOUT:
            return found, False
        try:
            daemon = json.loads(meta.read_text()).get("daemon") or {}
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            continue
        pid = daemon.get("pid")
        if isinstance(pid, int) and pid in unknown and daemon.get("state") in LIVE_DAEMON_STATES:
            owner = daemon.get("owner")
            found[pid] = owner if isinstance(owner, str) and UUID_RE.fullmatch(owner) else ""
    return found, True


def is_omp_worker(pid: int) -> bool:
    """An omp process that runs one of omp's own workers (`omp __omp_worker_<name>`), not a session."""
    return any(word.startswith("__omp_worker") for word in proc_words(pid, "cmdline")[1:3])


def in_family(cgroup: str, app: str, unit: str) -> bool:
    """Whether `cgroup` is `unit` in app.slice, or one of its `<unit>-N.scope` successors."""
    if not cgroup.startswith(app):
        return False
    leaf = cgroup[len(app):]
    stem = unit[: -len(".scope")]
    return leaf == unit or bool(re.fullmatch(re.escape(stem) + r"-[0-9]+\.scope", leaf))


def broker_scope_pass(state, options: argparse.Namespace, deadline: float = float("inf")) -> str:
    """Move each host worker broker into its own scope, and its services into their owners'."""
    dry_run = options.dry_run
    uid = os.getuid()
    user = f"/user.slice/user-{uid}.slice/"
    app = f"{user}user@{uid}.service/app.slice/"
    records: dict = state.data.setdefault("brokers", {})
    ticks = os.sysconf("SC_CLK_TCK")
    try:
        uptime = float((PROC_ROOT / "uptime").read_text().split()[0])
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
        uptime = 0.0
    brokers = []
    # A broker renames itself (comm `omp daemon brok`), so omp_processes never lists it.
    for entry in os.listdir(PROC_ROOT):
        if not entry.isdigit():
            continue
        pid = int(entry)
        try:
            if not (PROC_ROOT / entry / "comm").read_text().startswith("omp"):
                continue
        except OSError:
            continue
        cgroup = cgroup_of(pid)
        # This user's shared host brokers: in a session scope, or in the login or tmux scope of a
        # session that started without one. A broker in an agent box (a docker cgroup) is the
        # box's, and one whose runtime dir is outside ~/.omp belongs to a sandbox with a HOME of
        # its own (a grading jail's `env -i HOME=...`): its scope is that sandbox's limit, so it stays.
        if cgroup.startswith(user) and BROKER_ARG in proc_words(pid, "cmdline")[1:3]:
            env = dict(w.split("=", 1) for w in proc_words(pid, "environ") if "=" in w)
            if env.get("OMP_DAEMON_RUNTIME_DIR", "").startswith(OMP_HOME):
                brokers.append((pid, cgroup, env))
    live = {str(pid) for pid, _, _ in brokers}
    for key in set(records) - live:
        records.pop(key)
    children = children_by_parent() if brokers else {}
    created: set[str] = set()
    delegated: dict[str, bool] = {}
    moved = failed = 0
    notes: list[str] = []

    def place(unit: str, description: str, ceiling: int, pids: list[int]) -> None:
        nonlocal moved, failed
        pids = list(pids)
        if dry_run:
            log(f"broker scopes: would move {len(pids)} process(es) into {unit} ({description})")
            moved += len(pids)
            return
        # systemd adds a process to a running scope only when the scope is delegated ("Process
        # migration not available on non-delegated units"), and Delegate cannot be set on a
        # running unit. So a scope of this name without it (made before Delegate was asked for)
        # keeps what it holds, and new processes go to the next free `<name>-N.scope`.
        base, number = unit, 2
        while True:
            if unit in created:
                break
            if not (CGROUP_ROOT / app.strip("/") / unit).exists():
                props = ["Description", "s", description, "Slice", "s", "app.slice", "Delegate", "b", "true",
                         "OOMPolicy", "s", "continue", "MemoryMax", "t", str(ceiling), "MemorySwapMax", "t", "0"]
                while pids:
                    first = pids.pop(0)
                    ok, out = systemd_user(BUS + ["StartTransientUnit", "ssa(sv)a(sa(sv))", unit, "fail",
                                                  str(len(props) // 3 + 1), *props, "PIDs", "au", "1", str(first), "0"])
                    if ok:
                        moved += 1
                        break
                    if (PROC_ROOT / str(first)).exists():
                        log(f"broker scopes: FAILED to create {unit} for pid {first}: {out}")
                        notes.append(f"{unit}: create failed")
                        failed += 1
                        return
                else:
                    return
                break
            if unit not in delegated:
                ok, out = systemd_user(["systemctl", "--user", "show", "-p", "Delegate", "--value", unit])
                delegated[unit] = ok and out.strip() == "yes"
            if delegated[unit]:
                break
            unit, number = f"{base[:-len('.scope')]}-{number}.scope", number + 1
        created.add(unit)
        done, gone, left = attach(unit, pids, deadline)
        moved, failed = moved + done, failed + gone
        if left:
            notes.append(f"{unit}: {left} left for the next run")

    for broker, cgroup, env in brokers:
        if time.monotonic() > deadline - BUS_TIMEOUT:
            notes.append("out of time")
            break
        runtime_dir = env["OMP_DAEMON_RUNTIME_DIR"]
        record = records.get(str(broker))
        if not record or record.get("start") != pid_start(broker):
            record = records[str(broker)] = {"start": pid_start(broker), "from": [], "daemons": {}}
        own = broker_unit(runtime_dir)
        if not in_family(cgroup, app, own) and cgroup not in record["from"]:
            record["from"].append(cgroup)
        # The cgroups a process of this broker's tree is in only because it was forked there.
        inherited = set(record["from"]) | {cgroup}
        ceiling = ceiling_bytes(env.get("OMP_MEMORY_SCOPE", "")) or default_ceiling()

        # Who each direct child of the broker serves: a service's owner, "" for the broker's own.
        known: dict = record["daemons"]
        roots = children.get(broker, [])
        for key in set(known) - {str(p) for p in roots}:
            known.pop(key)
        unknown = {p for p in roots if known.get(str(p), [None])[0] != pid_start(p)}
        # A service runs with its owner's environment, so its OMP_SESSION_ID names the top-level
        # session that owns it: the one omp process and Envoy session a kill notice can reach.
        # The broker's record names the session or subagent that asked (a task subagent's own
        # id, 01a19db5 for a service of 01a14126's subagent BuildClock, 2026-11-03). The environment
        # is not enough when it carries the broker's own id: the broker carries the id of the
        # session that started it, and so does every process it runs for nobody (the headless
        # browser). Only those are looked up in the broker's records, to tell owned from nobody's;
        # reading them cost 8.6 s for 1,020 records at load 400.
        broker_sid = env.get("OMP_SESSION_ID", "")
        env_sid: dict[int, str] = {}
        ask: set[int] = set()
        for pid in unknown:
            sid = dict(w.split("=", 1) for w in proc_words(pid, "environ") if "=" in w).get("OMP_SESSION_ID", "")
            env_sid[pid] = sid if UUID_RE.fullmatch(sid) else ""
            if env_sid[pid] and sid != broker_sid:
                known[str(pid)] = [pid_start(pid), sid]
            else:
                ask.add(pid)
        found, complete = broker_daemons(runtime_dir, ask, deadline) if ask else ({}, True)
        unplaced: set[int] = set()
        for pid in ask:
            if pid in found:
                known[str(pid)] = [pid_start(pid), (env_sid[pid] or found[pid]) if found[pid] else ""]
            elif complete and uptime - pid_start(pid) / ticks >= ADOPT_MIN_AGE:
                known[str(pid)] = [pid_start(pid), ""]  # up a minute with no record: the broker's own
            else:
                unplaced.add(pid)  # its record not read yet, or not written yet: next run

        plan: dict[str, list[int]] = {}
        owners: dict[str, str] = {}
        if not in_family(cgroup, app, own):
            plan[own] = [broker]  # first: its scope must exist before its own children join it
        for root in roots:
            if root in unplaced:
                continue
            owner = known[str(root)][1]
            unit = service_unit(owner) if owner else own
            if owner:
                owners[unit] = owner
            stack = [root]
            while stack:
                pid = stack.pop()
                stack.extend(children.get(pid, []))
                where = cgroup_of(pid)
                if where in inherited and not in_family(where, app, unit):
                    plan.setdefault(unit, []).append(pid)
        project = env.get("OMP_DAEMON_PROJECT_DIR", runtime_dir)
        if own in plan:
            place(own, f"omp worker broker for {project}", ceiling, plan.pop(own))
        for unit, pids in plan.items():
            place(unit, f"omp session {owners[unit]}", default_ceiling(), pids)
    state.commit(options)
    verb = "would move" if dry_run else "moved"
    return (f"brokers: {len(brokers)}, {verb} {moved} process(es)" + (f", {failed} failed" if failed else "")
            + (f" ({'; '.join(notes)})" if notes else ""))


# ------------------------------------------------------------ resume a pane whose omp died

# What a resumed pane runs. The shim by its absolute path: a pane that starts omp from PATH can
# miss PI_CONFIG_FILES, which broke three sessions on 2026-11-01.
OMP_SHIM = Path.home() / ".dotfiles/shims/omp"
AGENTBOX = Path.home() / ".dotfiles/scripts/agentbox"
# A pane last seen running omp longer ago than this died while nothing watched; it is left.
RESTART_STALE = 3600.0
# How long a pane's session, read from the omp's open files, is reused while the same process runs.
SID_REFRESH = 300.0
# How long a recorded pane may run something other than omp before it is forgotten.
BUSY_FORGET = 120.0
# When each acting pass must stop, in seconds from the run's start. At nice 15 and load 425 on
# 2026-11-02 an unbounded pane pass took 33 s and the unit (then 25 s) killed the run. The
# alerting itself is not bounded; a pass out of time stops, and the next run goes on.
BIAS_DEADLINE = 6.0
RESTART_DEADLINE = 14.0
# Open-file scans a run may make: about 0.2 s each at that load.
SCAN_BUDGET = 10
# The window id (@N) as well as the target: with renumber-windows on, as on sami-rocqperf, a
# window's index changes when one before it closes, and a new pane for a session whose pane is
# gone goes into the window by its id.
PANE_FORMAT = "#{pane_id}\t#{pane_pid}\t#{pane_dead}\t#{session_name}:#{window_index}.#{pane_index}\t#{window_id}"
BOX_NAME_RE = re.compile(r"--name[=\s]+(agentbox-[0-9a-f]{12})\b")
PANE_WALK_DEPTH = 4
PANE_WALK_MAX = 64

_SIBLINGS: dict = {}


def sibling(name: str):
    """Load a script beside this one as a module, once: the discovery and the exit test are
    tmux-resurrect-omp's and envoy-role-holder-watch's, never a second copy."""
    if name not in _SIBLINGS:
        path = Path(SELF_PATH).parent / name
        loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader(name.replace("-", "_"), str(path))
        spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader(loader.name, loader)
        module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
        loader.exec_module(module)
        _SIBLINGS[name] = module
    return _SIBLINGS[name]


def tmux_panes() -> list[dict] | None:
    """Every pane of the default tmux server, or None when there is no server to ask."""
    try:
        done = subprocess.run(
            ["tmux", "list-panes", "-a", "-F", PANE_FORMAT],
            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None
    if done.returncode != 0:
        return None
    rows = []
    for line in done.stdout.splitlines():
        parts = line.split("\t")
        if len(parts) == 5 and parts[1].isdigit():
            rows.append({"id": parts[0], "pid": int(parts[1]), "dead": parts[2] == "1", "target": parts[3], "window": parts[4]})
    return rows


def process_table() -> dict:
    """Every process on the machine from one read of each /proc/<pid>/stat: its children, its
    name and its start time.

    tmux-resurrect-omp's `children` reads /proc/<pid>/task/*/children, one file per THREAD, and
    an omp has dozens: walking 273 panes that way took 7.5 of a 9.3 s run at load 400 on
    2026-11-02. At that load each /proc open costs about 1 ms, so the walk reads nothing else per
    process, and a command line (the slowest file: it takes the process's mmap lock) only where
    a decision needs it.
    """
    children: dict[int, list[int]] = {}
    names: dict[int, str] = {}
    starts: dict[int, int] = {}
    for entry in os.listdir(PROC_ROOT):
        if not entry.isdigit():
            continue
        try:
            data = (PROC_ROOT / entry / "stat").read_text()
            fields = data[data.rfind(")") + 2 :].split()
            parent, start = int(fields[1]), int(fields[19])
        except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
            continue
        pid = int(entry)
        names[pid] = data[data.find("(") + 1 : data.rfind(")")]
        starts[pid] = start
        children.setdefault(parent, []).append(pid)
    return {"children": children, "names": names, "starts": starts}


def pane_processes(pane_pid: int, resurrect, table: dict | None = None) -> list[tuple[int, str]]:
    """(pid, comm) of the processes below the pane's own process, nearest first.

    The walk stops at an omp: what runs below it is that session's tools, not the pane's. From
    `table` when given (one /proc pass for the whole run), else read fresh."""
    found: list[tuple[int, str]] = []
    frontier = [pane_pid]
    for _ in range(PANE_WALK_DEPTH):
        next_frontier: list[int] = []
        for pid in frontier:
            kids = table["children"].get(pid, []) if table is not None else resurrect.children(pid)
            for kid in kids:
                name = table["names"].get(kid, "") if table is not None else resurrect.comm(kid)
                found.append((kid, name))
                if len(found) >= PANE_WALK_MAX:
                    return found
                if name != "omp":
                    next_frontier.append(kid)
        frontier = next_frontier
    return found


def cwd_of(pid: int) -> str:
    try:
        return os.readlink(f"/proc/{pid}/cwd")
    except OSError:
        return ""


def pane_state(
    pane: dict, resurrect, known: dict | None = None, now: float = 0.0, table: dict | None = None,
    budget: dict | None = None,
) -> tuple[str, dict]:
    """("host" | "box" | "idle" | "busy", what was read) for one pane.

    host: an omp runs below the pane. box: an `agentbox new ... --` launcher does (its omp runs
    in the box under another uid). idle: nothing runs below the pane's shell, or the pane is
    dead. busy: something else runs there. The session is found the way tmux-resurrect-omp finds
    it: the session file the omp holds open, else the id on its command line.

    The open-file scan is the expensive part (6.4 s for 171 host panes on 2026-11-02, 30 s at
    nice 15 and load 425), so the pane's last record (`known`) is reused while the same omp
    process runs and its session was read less than SID_REFRESH ago, and a run scans at most
    `budget["scans"]` panes; a pane over the budget keeps what it had, else the id on its
    command line, and is scanned on a later run. A session switched in-app (/new) less than
    SID_REFRESH before its omp was killed is resumed at the session it ran before the switch.

    The flags the session was launched with are read here, from the live process's own argv,
    because nothing can read them once it is dead: `flags` is a list, or None when they could
    not be read, with `flags_why` saying why. They are read once per omp process -- an argv does
    not change -- and are carried forward only while the SAME process (pid and start) runs, so a
    record never carries another process's flags; a pane whose read failed is read again on a
    later run, at most `budget["flags"]` reads a run.
    """
    if pane["dead"]:
        return "idle", {}
    procs = pane_processes(pane["pid"], resurrect, table)
    if not procs:
        return "idle", {}
    starts = table["starts"] if table is not None else {}
    for pid, name in procs:
        if name != "omp":
            continue
        start = starts.get(pid) or pid_start(pid)
        same = bool(known) and known.get("kind") == "host" and known.get("pid") == pid and known.get("start") == start
        resolved = float(known.get("resolved") or 0) if same else 0.0
        sid = known.get("sid") if same else None
        if not (sid and now - resolved < SID_REFRESH):
            deadline = (budget or {}).get("deadline", float("inf"))
            if (budget is None or budget["scans"] > 0) and time.monotonic() < deadline - 1:
                if budget is not None:
                    budget["scans"] -= 1
                sid, resolved = resurrect.open_session(pid) or resurrect.named_session(resurrect.cmdline(pid)), now
            elif not sid:
                sid = resurrect.named_session(resurrect.cmdline(pid))
        cwd = known.get("cwd") if same and known.get("cwd") else cwd_of(pid)
        flags, flags_why = kept_flags(known if same else None, lambda: proc_words(pid, "cmdline"), budget)
        return "host", {"pid": pid, "start": start, "sid": sid, "resolved": resolved, "cwd": cwd,
                        "memory_max": own_scope_memory_max(pid), "flags": flags, "flags_why": flags_why}
    for pid, _name in procs:
        command = resurrect.cmdline(pid)
        launch = resurrect.LAUNCH_SPEC.search(command)
        if launch:
            box = next(
                (m.group(1) for p, _n in procs if (m := BOX_NAME_RE.search(resurrect.cmdline(p)))), ""
            )
            start = starts.get(pid) or pid_start(pid)
            same = bool(known) and known.get("kind") == "box" and known.get("pid") == pid and known.get("start") == start
            flags, flags_why = kept_flags(
                known if same else None, lambda: box_omp_words(proc_words(pid, "cmdline")), budget
            )
            return "box", {
                "pid": pid, "start": start, "sid": resurrect.named_session(command),
                "spec": launch.group("spec"), "box": box, "cwd": cwd_of(pid),
                "flags": flags, "flags_why": flags_why,
            }
    return "busy", {"comm": procs[0][1]}


def find_transcript(sid: str, holder) -> str:
    """The newest transcript of a session id (or id prefix), else ""."""
    found = sorted(SESSION_ROOT.glob(f"*/*_{sid}*.jsonl"), key=lambda p: p.stat().st_mtime if p.exists() else 0)
    if found:
        return str(found[-1])
    if UUID_RE.fullmatch(sid):
        path = holder.transcript_of(sid)
        if path:
            return str(path)
    return ""


def same_session(a: str, b: str) -> bool:
    return bool(a and b) and (a.startswith(b) or b.startswith(a))


def live_holder(sid: str, omps: list[tuple[int, str]], resurrect, records: dict, pane_id: str, now: float) -> str:
    """Who else runs this session, else "": another pane this watch saw running it within
    SID_REFRESH (read from that omp's open files), or a live omp whose command line names it.

    No open-file scan here: one per omp is about 0.2 s at load 400, a hundred omps would cost the
    whole run, and the pane records already hold what those scans found."""
    for other, record in records.items():
        if (
            other != pane_id and not record.get("gone_at") and now - float(record.get("seen") or 0) < SID_REFRESH
            and same_session(record.get("sid") or "", sid)
        ):
            return f"pane {other}"
    for pid, _cgroup in omps:
        if same_session(resurrect.named_session(cmdline_of(pid)) or "", sid):
            return f"omp pid {pid}"
    return ""


RESUME_WAKE_NOTICE = (
    "omp-memory-watch: this session's omp ended without a normal exit, so its pane was resumed."
    " Go on with the work you were doing. (An automated notice, not a person.)"
)


def own_scope_memory_max(pid: int) -> int | None:
    """The memory.max of the transient scope this omp runs in, read each pass, else None.

    A resume through `shims/omp` takes its host-scaled default unless OMP_MEMORY_MAX says otherwise, so a
    session whose scope had been raised came back lower than it was using: 01a19336 ran at a
    40 GiB ceiling, was resumed into 24 GiB on 2026-11-02 at 09:18:48Z, and its last peak was
    34 GiB. Only a `run-*.scope` of its own counts: a session in the login scope has no ceiling to
    carry, and its scope's memory.max is not the session's. Read each pass, so a ceiling raised
    by hand while the session runs is the one carried.
    """
    try:
        cgroup = Path(f"/proc/{pid}/cgroup").read_text().strip().rpartition(":")[2]
    except OSError:
        return None
    if not re.fullmatch(r"run-[^/]+\.scope", cgroup.rpartition("/")[2]):
        return None
    return read_int(CGROUP_ROOT / cgroup.strip("/") / "memory.max")


def gone_box_dirs(spec: str) -> list[str]:
    """Every --dir of an `agentbox new` spec after the first that is no longer a directory.

    A spec is what the box was given when it started, and a path in it can be gone by the time
    the session is resumed: 01a1798a's box spec of 2026-11-02T10:42Z named its old omp's TMPDIR,
    /mnt/nvme/sami/session-tmp/53458-LivaHYZv, which is removed when that omp exits, and at its
    16:23Z resume `agentbox new` refused it as "not an existing directory" and the pane sat at a
    shell. The launcher leaves such a path out (--drop-gone-dirs), and a FILE as well (a re-box's
    spec named /mnt/nvme/sami/tmp/ossv172-codex-turns.md on 2026-11-04); this names them for the log.
    The first --dir is where the box works, the record's cwd, which restart_decision checks."""
    words = spec.split()
    dirs = [words[i + 1] for i in range(len(words) - 1) if words[i] == "--dir"]
    return [d for d in dirs[1:] if not os.path.isdir(d)]


def gone_note(resume: dict) -> str:
    """The log's words for the --dir paths a resume goes on without, else ""."""
    gone = resume.get("gone_dirs") or []
    if not gone:
        return ""
    return "; without " + ", ".join(f"--dir {d}" for d in gone) + (", which is no longer a directory" if len(gone) == 1 else ", which are no longer directories")


# ------------------------------------------------------------ the flags a session was launched with

# A resume used to be a bare `omp --resume <id>`, so a session came back on another
# configuration than it ran on: the lane 01a15237 ran as `omp --profile pbc --model
# pbc-gateway/pbc/claude-opus-5-5 --resume <jsonl>` and this watch resumed its pane with none of
# it on 2026-11-04 at 16:52Z. `agentbox move` fixed the same defect in its own respawn
# (scripts/agentbox move_omp_flags) by reading the omp's own argv; this reads it too.
#
# Nothing here lists the flags to keep. Telling a flag's value from a positional word (omp
# records a positional as a message the person typed) needs each flag's arity, which is
# `relaunchArgv` in scripts/agentbox-run.ts -- the box runner's restart rule, and the one
# scripts/agentbox asks through bun. A second copy of that rule would drift, so this asks the
# same function the same way.
AGENTBOX_RUN_TS = Path.home() / ".dotfiles/scripts/agentbox-run.ts"
LAUNCH_FLAGS_JS = """
const { relaunchArgv } = await import(process.env.AGENTBOX_RUN_TS);
const words = (await Bun.stdin.text()).split("\\0");
if (words.at(-1) === "") words.pop();
// argv[0] is the omp executable; relaunchArgv keeps it first and ends with `--resume <sid>`.
const kept = relaunchArgv(["omp", ...words.slice(1)], "-").slice(1, -2);
process.stdout.write(kept.map(w => `${w}\\0`).join(""));
"""
# Flag reads a run may make: about 30 ms each (bun 1.4.4, sami-rocqperf, 2026-11-04), and only
# for a pane whose omp process this watch has not read yet, so a steady box makes none.
FLAG_READS = 12
_BUN: str | None = None


def bun_path() -> str:
    """bun's real path, "" when this machine has none. Looked up once a run.

    A mise SHIM is not it: the unit's PATH has `~/.mise/shims` on it, and running the shim
    INSTALLS the pinned bun when the machine has not got it (measured here on 2026-11-04: 1.38 s
    and a download, against 18 ms for the installed binary). `mise which` only resolves, as
    scripts/secret-run does for the same reason.
    """
    global _BUN
    if _BUN is None:
        found = shutil.which("bun") or ""
        if not found or f"{os.sep}shims{os.sep}" in found:
            try:
                done = subprocess.run(["mise", "which", "bun"], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10,
                                      stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL)
                resolved = done.stdout.strip() if done.returncode == 0 else ""
            except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
                resolved = ""
            found = resolved or (found if f"{os.sep}shims{os.sep}" not in found else "")
        _BUN = found
    return _BUN


def launch_flags(words: list[str], budget: dict | None = None) -> tuple[list[str] | None, str]:
    """(the flags `words` was launched with, "") for an omp argv, else (None, why not).

    `words` is a whole argv, argv[0] included. What comes back is every flag except the session
    source (--resume, --session, --continue, --fork, …; a resume names its own) and the
    positional words. None is NOT an empty flag list: it means they could not be read, and the
    caller must say so rather than resume as though the session had none.
    """
    if len(words) < 2:
        return ([], "") if words else (None, "its command line could not be read")
    bun = bun_path()
    if not bun:
        return None, ("no bun on PATH (nor through mise), which reads the launch flags"
                      " (scripts/agentbox-run.ts relaunchArgv)")
    if budget is not None:
        if budget.get("flags", 0) <= 0:
            return None, "this run's flag reads were spent; the next run reads them"
        budget["flags"] -= 1
    try:
        done = subprocess.run(
            [bun, "-e", LAUNCH_FLAGS_JS], input="\0".join(words).encode("utf8", "replace"),
            capture_output=True, timeout=20, env=dict(os.environ, AGENTBOX_RUN_TS=str(AGENTBOX_RUN_TS)),
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as failure:
        return None, f"reading the launch flags with {bun} failed ({failure})"
    if done.returncode != 0:
        return None, (f"reading the launch flags with {bun} and scripts/agentbox-run.ts failed"
                      f" (exit {done.returncode})")
    kept = done.stdout.decode("utf8", "replace").split("\0")
    if kept and kept[-1] == "":
        kept.pop()
    return kept, ""


def box_omp_words(words: list[str]) -> list[str]:
    """The omp argv of an `agentbox new <spec> -- omp <flags> --resume <id>` launcher line, [] if
    it has no `--`. The launcher runs on the host, so its own argv holds the flags its box's omp
    was given; the omp itself runs in the box under another uid and is not in this /proc."""
    return words[words.index("--") + 1 :] if "--" in words else []


def kept_flags(known: dict | None, words, budget: dict | None) -> tuple[list[str] | None, str]:
    """(the flags of the omp a pane runs, "") or (None, why not), reading its argv at most once.

    `known` is the pane's last record ONLY when the same process still runs there, so flags are
    carried forward and never belong to a process that has since been replaced. `words` is
    called only when there is nothing to carry: an argv read costs a /proc read and a bun run.
    """
    if known is not None and known.get("flags") is not None:
        return known["flags"], ""
    return launch_flags(words(), budget)



def flags_note(resume: dict) -> str:
    """The log's words for a resume that carries no launch flags, else ""."""
    if resume.get("flags") is not None:
        return ""
    why = resume.get("flags_why") or "they could not be read"
    return f"; WITHOUT the flags it was launched with ({why}), so it comes back on omp's defaults"


def resume_command(record: dict) -> str:
    """What the lead runs by hand, with the launcher by its absolute path.

    The session comes back with the flags it was launched with (`flags`, read from its own argv
    while it was alive; launch_flags), since a bare `omp --resume <id>` brings it back on another
    profile, model or configuration than it ran on. A record whose flags could NOT be read has
    None there, and the resume carries none rather than a guess -- the log and the report say so
    (flags_note).

    The resume carries no prompt: omp records a positional as a user message, and the `.` this
    used to end with was counted as text the person typed by the transcript store. The session is
    woken instead by one Envoy notice, sent by `agentbox move --wake` once the resumed process has
    registered; it runs in the background beside the launcher and logs to wake-<id>.log."""
    cwd, sid = shlex.quote(record["cwd"]), shlex.quote(record["sid"])
    flags = "".join(f" {shlex.quote(word)}" for word in (record.get("flags") or []))
    log = shlex.quote(str(Path.home() / ".cache" / "omp" / "agentbox" / f"wake-{record['sid']}.log"))
    wake = (
        f"( setsid {shlex.quote(str(AGENTBOX))} move --wake {sid} {shlex.quote(RESUME_WAKE_NOTICE)}"
        f" </dev/null >>{log} 2>&1 & ); "
    )
    if record["kind"] == "box":
        # --drop-gone-dirs: a --dir removed since the box started is left out, not refused
        # (box_spec_dirs). A spec read from a launcher a move started has it already.
        drop = "" if "--drop-gone-dirs" in record["spec"].split() else "--drop-gone-dirs "
        return f"{wake}cd {cwd} && {shlex.quote(str(AGENTBOX))} new {drop}{record['spec']} -- omp{flags} --resume {sid}"
    ceiling = record.get("memory_max")
    env = f"OMP_MEMORY_MAX={int(ceiling)} " if isinstance(ceiling, int) and ceiling > 0 else ""
    return f"{wake}cd {cwd} && {env}{shlex.quote(str(OMP_SHIM))}{flags} --resume {sid}"


def pane_now(pane_id: str) -> dict | None:
    """The pane as tmux has it at this moment: its process and whether it is dead, or None."""
    try:
        done = subprocess.run(
            ["tmux", "display-message", "-p", "-t", pane_id, "#{pane_pid}\t#{pane_dead}"],
            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None
    parts = done.stdout.strip().split("\t")
    if done.returncode != 0 or len(parts) != 2 or not parts[0].isdigit():
        return None
    return {"pid": int(parts[0]), "dead": parts[1] == "1"}


def respawn_pane(pane_id: str, cwd: str, command: str) -> tuple[bool, str]:
    argv = ["tmux", "respawn-pane", "-k", "-t", pane_id, "-c", cwd, "bash", "-i", "-c", f"{command}; exec bash -i"]
    try:
        done = subprocess.run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as error:
        return False, str(error)
    return done.returncode == 0, done.stderr.strip()


def tmux_call(argv: list[str]) -> tuple[bool, str]:
    """Run one tmux command: (it succeeded, its stdout, else its error)."""
    try:
        done = subprocess.run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as failure:
        return False, str(failure)
    return done.returncode == 0, (done.stdout if done.returncode == 0 else done.stderr).strip()


def open_pane(window: str, session: str, name: str, cwd: str, command: str) -> tuple[bool, str, str]:
    """Open a new pane for `command`: (True, its pane id, where it is) or (False, each error, "").

    In `window`, as scripts/agent-new opens a session's pane (`split-window -d`, and once more
    after `select-layout tiled` when tmux has no space for it). When the window has no room even
    then, which is common here (the agents windows hold 10 and more panes, and a drill window
    of 80x24 refused a third split on 2026-11-02 at 15:29:49Z), or is gone, a new window `name`
    in the tmux session `session`."""
    run = ["-c", cwd, "bash", "-i", "-c", f"{command}; exec bash -i"]
    errors = []
    if window:
        split = ["tmux", "split-window", "-d", "-P", "-F", "#{pane_id}", "-t", window, *run]
        ok, out = tmux_call(split)
        if not ok and "no space for" in out:
            tmux_call(["tmux", "select-layout", "-t", window, "tiled"])
            ok, out = tmux_call(split)
        if ok:
            return True, out, f"window {window}"
        errors.append(f"split-window -t {window}: {out}")
    if session:
        ok, out = tmux_call(["tmux", "new-window", "-d", "-P", "-F", "#{pane_id}", "-t", f"{session}:", "-n", name, *run])
        if ok:
            return True, out, f"new window {name} of tmux session {session}"
        errors.append(f"new-window -t {session}: {out}")
    return False, "; ".join(errors) or "no window or tmux session was recorded", ""


# ------------------------------------------------------------ who brings a session back

# `agentbox move` hangs a session's omp up (SIGHUP, so its transcript ends in a `signal`
# session_exit like any other) and then respawns the pane into the box itself, 1 to 17 s later
# on 2026-11-02, or after up to 40 failed tries 15 s apart. Its detached relauncher logs each step
# here, one file per session, appended to by every move of that session.
MOVE_LOG_DIR = Path.home() / ".cache" / "omp" / "agentbox"
# How long after its latest step a move still owns the session's respawn. A move that logs
# nothing for this long has died without saying how it ended.
MOVE_OWNS = 600.0
# A log line is stamped to the second and a pane is read up to RESTART_DEADLINE after the run's
# start, which is the `seen` a record holds, so a step this much before `seen` still counts.
MOVE_SLACK = 2.0
MOVE_LINE_RE = re.compile(r"(\d{4}-\d\d-\d\dT\d\d:\d\d:\d\dZ) move ([0-9a-f-]{36}): (.*)")
# The first line of each move (move_relaunch): `resume <path>` is absent before 2026-11-02.
MOVE_START_RE = re.compile(r"pid \d+, pane (%\d+), cwd (.+?), (?:resume (.+?), )?spec (.*)")
# The steps of a move still in progress, after its first line: the hang-up, the teardown, each
# respawn try, and the respawn itself (move_respawn; the launcher typed into the pane's shell is
# its last resort). The Envoy wake lines after them are the resumed session's, not the move's.
MOVE_STEPS = (
    "hung up; waiting for its teardown", "exited after", "is still up after", "respawn of pane",
    "respawned into", "the launcher was typed into",
)
# The move's last line when it gave up. "is DOWN": every respawn try failed. "is gone": the pane
# closed with its omp, so there was nothing to respawn (pane %74 of 01a16c67 on 2026-11-02).
# "respawn pane failed" is tmux's own error, printed unstamped by the relauncher before 2026-11-02.
MOVE_GAVE_UP = ("the session is DOWN", "is gone; nothing respawned")

# `agentbox move --to <machine>` moves a session to ANOTHER machine. While its relauncher runs it
# keeps <MOVE_LOG_DIR>/move-to/active/<session id>, lines `pid=<relauncher pid>`,
# `target=<machine>`, `start=<epoch>`, and removes it when the move ends. On 2026-11-03 at
# 04:10:57Z this watch resumed 01a19e3c on sami-rocqperf 6 s after its move to sami-devbox hung it
# up, since the same-machine move log above says nothing of such a move.
#
# Once moved, the session runs on the target, and one session must never run on two machines
# (dispatch://INFRA-993): the move leaves the marker `agentbox-moved` in the session's own
# directory (its transcript path without `.jsonl`) and as MOVED_INDEX/<session id>, lines
# `from=`, `to=`, `time=`. As in scripts/agent-session, it counts only on the machine its `from=`
# names, so a copy that reaches the target is inert there.
MOVED_MARKER = "agentbox-moved"
MOVED_INDEX = Path.home() / ".omp" / "agentbox-moved"


def key_values(path: Path) -> dict[str, str]:
    """A file of `key=value` lines as a dict, or {} when it cannot be read."""
    try:
        text = path.read_text(errors="replace")
    except OSError:
        return {}
    return dict(line.split("=", 1) for line in text.splitlines() if "=" in line)


def move_to_claim(sid: str) -> dict:
    """{"owner": "move", "at", "line", "why"} while an `agentbox move --to` of the session runs
    here: its active file is present and its relauncher is alive. {} otherwise, so a move whose
    relauncher died without removing the file holds nothing."""
    directory = MOVE_LOG_DIR / "move-to" / "active"
    path = directory / sid
    if not path.exists():
        found = sorted(directory.glob(f"{sid}*"))
        if len(found) != 1:
            return {}
        path = found[0]
    fields = key_values(path)
    pid = fields.get("pid", "")
    if not pid.isdigit() or not (PROC_ROOT / pid).exists():
        return {}
    try:
        start = float(fields.get("start") or 0)
    except ValueError:
        start = 0.0
    target = fields.get("target") or "another machine"
    why = f"agentbox move --to {target} owns it (relauncher pid {pid}, since {iso(start)})"
    return {"owner": "move", "at": start, "line": why, "why": why}


def moved_away(marker: Path) -> str:
    """Why the session must not be resumed here, when `marker` says it moved from this machine."""
    fields = key_values(marker)
    here = os.environ.get("AGENTBOX_HOST") or socket.gethostname().split(".")[0]
    if not fields or fields.get("from") != here:
        return ""
    return (
        f"it moved to {fields.get('to') or 'another machine'} at {fields.get('time') or 'an unknown time'}"
        f" (marker {marker}) and runs there now"
    )


def owner_text(claim: dict) -> str:
    """Who owns the session's respawn, in the words a `wait:` or `kept:` line uses."""
    return claim.get("why") or f"agentbox move owns its respawn ({iso(claim['at'])}: {claim['line'][:120]})"


def who_respawns(sid: str, since: float, now: float) -> dict:
    """Whether something other than this watch brings the session back. The one place that
    decides it.

    {"owner": "move", "at", "line"}: an `agentbox move` is respawning the session, here or (with
    "why") on another machine (`move_to_claim`), so a resume now would give one transcript two
    writers. {"owner": "down", "at", "line", "record"}: the same-machine move gave up, and
    `record` is what it was moving the session to (kind "box", its spec), which is what the
    session is brought back as. {} when nothing else claims it.

    Only the session's latest move counts, and only its lines from `since` on (the record's last
    sight of the session running): a move this watch saw the session survive is history.

    A `signal` session_exit cannot tell a move from a machine loss, a lead's restart or a memory
    stop, which all must be resumed (dispatch://INFRA-898, about 55 of 73 read). Today the move's
    own log says it; when omp records the sender's `intent` in session_exit.data, it is read here.
    """
    claim = move_to_claim(sid)
    if claim:
        return claim
    paths = sorted(MOVE_LOG_DIR.glob(f"move-{sid}*.log"))
    if len(paths) != 1:
        return {}
    try:
        lines = paths[0].read_text(errors="replace").splitlines()
    except OSError:
        return {}
    begun: dict | None = None
    last: dict = {}
    stamp = 0.0
    for raw in lines:
        match = MOVE_LINE_RE.match(raw)
        if not match:
            if begun is not None and raw.startswith("respawn pane failed"):
                last = {"owner": "down", "at": stamp, "line": raw}
            continue
        stamp = float(calendar.timegm(time.strptime(match.group(1), "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")))
        line = match.group(3)
        start = MOVE_START_RE.fullmatch(line)
        if start:
            begun = {"kind": "box", "sid": match.group(2), "pane": start.group(1), "cwd": start.group(2),
                     "spec": start.group(4)}
            last = {"owner": "move", "at": stamp, "line": line}
        elif begun is None:
            continue
        elif any(words in line for words in MOVE_GAVE_UP):
            last = {"owner": "down", "at": stamp, "line": line}
        elif any(words in line for words in MOVE_STEPS):
            last = {"owner": "move", "at": stamp, "line": line}
    if not last or begun is None or last["at"] < since - MOVE_SLACK:
        return {}
    if last["owner"] == "move":
        return last if now - last["at"] < MOVE_OWNS else {}
    return dict(last, record=begun)


def restart_decision(record: dict, omps: list, restarts: dict, options: argparse.Namespace, now: float,
                     resurrect, holder, records: dict, pane_id: str) -> tuple[str, dict]:
    """("", what to resume) when the pane's session should be resumed, else (why it is left, {}).

    What to resume is the pane's record, or the box an `agentbox move` that gave up was moving
    the session to. The move's log names no launch flags, so a resume into that box carries the
    ones this watch read from the session's own omp while it ran. A reason that starts "wait:"
    keeps the record for the next run. Cheap tests first."""
    sid = record.get("sid") or ""
    if not sid:
        return "left: the session it ran could not be read while it was alive", {}
    claim = who_respawns(sid, float(record.get("seen") or 0), now)
    if claim.get("owner") == "move":
        return f"wait: {owner_text(claim)}", {}
    moved = moved_away(MOVED_INDEX / sid)
    if moved:
        return f"left: {moved}", {}
    resume = dict(claim["record"], flags=record.get("flags"), flags_why=record.get("flags_why") or "") if claim else record
    if now - float(record.get("seen") or 0) > RESTART_STALE:
        return f"left: last seen running at {iso(record.get('seen'))}, more than {human(RESTART_STALE)} ago", {}
    if not resume.get("cwd") or not os.path.isdir(resume["cwd"]):
        return f"left: its working directory {resume.get('cwd') or '(unknown)'} is gone", {}
    if resume.get("kind") == "box" and not resume.get("spec"):
        return "left: the agentbox launcher line names no spec", {}
    if resume.get("kind") == "box":
        resume = dict(resume, gone_dirs=gone_box_dirs(resume["spec"]))
    last = float(restarts.get(sid) or 0)
    if last and now - last < options.restart_cooldown:
        return f"left: resumed at {iso(last)}, less than {human(options.restart_cooldown)} ago", {}
    transcript = find_transcript(sid, holder)
    if not transcript:
        return "left: no transcript found, so whether it ended on purpose cannot be told", {}
    moved = moved_away(Path(transcript).with_suffix("") / MOVED_MARKER)
    if moved:
        return f"left: {moved}", {}
    ended = holder.normal_exit_at(transcript)
    if ended:
        return f"left: the session ended normally at {ended}", {}
    elsewhere = live_holder(sid, omps, resurrect, records, pane_id, now)
    if elsewhere:
        return f"left: the session is live in {elsewhere}", {}
    if claim:
        resume["gave_up"] = f"{iso(claim['at'])}: {claim['line'][:160]}"
    return "", resume


def resume_failed_text(sid: str, why: str, how: str) -> str:
    """The report of a session this watch decided to resume and could not: why, and the command."""
    return (
        f"omp-memory-watch: session {sid} is down and was NOT resumed: {why}. Resume it from a host"
        f" session: {how}"
    )


def resume_failed_head(sid: str, why: str) -> str:
    """The box-health owner's message for it: the session, why, and where the command is."""
    return (
        f"omp-memory-watch: session {sid} is down and was NOT resumed: {why[:300]}. Resume it from a"
        " host session with the command in the report."
    )


def down_resumed_text(sid: str, gave_up: str, where: str, command: str) -> str:
    """The report of a session `agentbox move` left down and this watch resumed."""
    return (
        f"omp-memory-watch: agentbox move could not bring session {sid} back ({gave_up}). This watch"
        f" resumed it {where}: {command}"
    )


def down_resumed_head(sid: str, gave_up: str, where: str) -> str:
    """The box-health owner's notice for it: which move gave up, and where the session runs now."""
    return (
        f"omp-memory-watch: agentbox move could not bring session {sid} back ({gave_up[:120]}). This"
        f" watch resumed it {where}; nothing to do unless it goes down again."
    )


def restart_pass(
    options: argparse.Namespace, state: State, omps: list[tuple[int, str]], now: float,
    deadline: float = float("inf"), envoy: Envoy | None = None,
) -> str:
    """Resume each tmux pane whose omp died without ending its session on purpose.

    Each run records, for every pane running omp (on the host or through `agentbox new`), the
    session it runs, its working directory, its window, the flags its omp was launched with
    (read from the live process's argv: a dead one has none to read) and, for a box, the
    launcher's spec. A
    recorded pane that is now idle -- its shell has no child, or tmux marks it dead -- is resumed
    with `tmux respawn-pane -k`, unless: no session was read for it; an `agentbox move` is
    respawning it (who_respawns: the record is kept and read again next run); it was last seen
    running more than an hour ago; its directory is gone; the session's transcript ends in a
    normal `session_exit` (envoy-role-holder-watch's normal_exit_at); another omp holds the
    session; or the session was resumed less than --restart-cooldown ago. A pane that is gone is
    forgotten, unless a move holds its session: then it is kept while the move runs, and when the
    move gives up the session is resumed in a new pane of the same window. Every decision is
    logged; a session this pass leaves down after deciding to resume it, and a session it brings
    back after a move gave up, are told to the box-health owner (`envoy`).
    """
    if not os.access(OMP_SHIM, os.X_OK):
        return f"panes: restarter off here, no {OMP_SHIM}"
    panes = tmux_panes()
    if panes is None:
        return "panes: no tmux server"
    resurrect = sibling("tmux-resurrect-omp")
    holder = sibling("envoy-role-holder-watch")
    dry = options.dry_run or options.restart_dry_run
    records: dict = state.data.setdefault("panes", {})
    restarts: dict = state.data.setdefault("restarts", {})
    for sid in [sid for sid, at in restarts.items() if now - float(at) > max(options.restart_cooldown, RESTART_STALE)]:
        restarts.pop(sid, None)
    live = resumed = left = waiting = 0
    box_lookups = 0
    table = process_table()
    budget = {"scans": SCAN_BUDGET, "flags": FLAG_READS, "deadline": deadline}
    present = {pane["id"] for pane in panes}

    def report(kind: str, head: str, body: str) -> str:
        """Tell the box-health owner `head`, with `body` as its report; say who heard it."""
        heard = ""
        if envoy is not None:
            heard = tell_box_health(envoy, short_message(head, write_report(options, kind, body, now)))
        return heard or "nobody: every route refused it"

    for pane_id in [pane_id for pane_id in records if pane_id not in present]:
        old = records[pane_id]
        name = f"pane {pane_id} ({old.get('target')}) session {old.get('sid') or 'unknown'}"
        # A pane its owner closed stays closed (dispatch://INFRA-898, group 6). A pane that closed
        # during a move is the move's: it owns the session until it respawns it or gives up.
        claim = who_respawns(old["sid"], float(old.get("seen") or 0), now) if old.get("sid") else {}
        if claim.get("owner") == "move":
            waiting += 1
            if not old.get("gone_at"):
                old["gone_at"] = now
                log(f"{name}: kept: the pane is gone, and {owner_text(claim)}")
            continue
        records.pop(pane_id)
        if not claim:
            log(f"{name}: forgotten, the pane is gone")
            continue
        reason, resume = restart_decision(old, omps, restarts, options, now, resurrect, holder, records, pane_id)
        if reason:
            log(f"{name}: the pane is gone; {reason}")
            left += 1
            continue
        window = old.get("window") or (old.get("target") or "").rpartition(".")[0]
        tmux_session = (old.get("target") or "").rpartition(":")[0]
        title = f"omp-{resume['sid'][:8]}"
        command = resume_command(resume)
        how = f"tmux new-window -n {title} -c {shlex.quote(resume['cwd'])} bash -i -c {shlex.quote(command + '; exec bash -i')}"
        if dry:
            log(f"{name}: DRY RUN, would resume in a new pane of window {window} (else a new window {title}"
                f" of tmux session {tmux_session}): agentbox move gave up ({resume['gave_up']}){gone_note(resume)}{flags_note(resume)}: {command}")
            resumed += 1
            continue
        restarts[resume["sid"]] = now
        state.commit(options)
        ok, out, where = open_pane(window, tmux_session, title, resume["cwd"], command)
        if ok:
            place = f"in new pane {out} ({where}), since pane {pane_id} is gone"
            heard = report(
                "resumed", down_resumed_head(resume["sid"], resume["gave_up"], place),
                down_resumed_text(resume["sid"], resume["gave_up"], place, command),
            )
            log(f"{name}: RESUMED in new pane {out} ({where}): agentbox move gave up ({resume['gave_up']}){gone_note(resume)}{flags_note(resume)}: {command} -> {heard}")
            resumed += 1
        else:
            why = f"agentbox move gave up ({resume['gave_up']}), its pane {pane_id} is gone, and no new pane could be opened: {out}"
            heard = report("resume-failed", resume_failed_head(resume["sid"], why), resume_failed_text(resume["sid"], why, how))
            log(f"{name}: no new pane could be opened: {out} -> {heard}")
            left += 1
    # A run that runs out of RESTART_BUDGET stops where it is, and the next run starts there, so
    # every pane is read within a few runs however loaded the box is and the watch's alerting
    # never waits on it.
    start = int(state.data.get("pane_cursor") or 0) % max(len(panes), 1)
    order = panes[start:] + panes[:start]
    unread = 0
    for index, pane in enumerate(order):
        if time.monotonic() > deadline:
            unread = len(order) - index
            state.data["pane_cursor"] = (start + index) % len(panes)
            break
        old = records.get(pane["id"])
        kind, seen = pane_state(pane, resurrect, old, now, table, budget)
        if kind in ("host", "box"):
            live += 1
            same_process = old and old.get("pid") == seen["pid"] and old.get("start") == seen["start"]
            if not seen["sid"] and same_process:
                seen["sid"] = old.get("sid")
            if kind == "box":
                seen["looked"] = old.get("looked", 0) if same_process else 0
            if (
                kind == "box" and not seen["sid"] and seen["box"] and box_lookups < 1
                and now - float(seen["looked"]) >= SID_REFRESH and deadline - time.monotonic() > 11.0
            ):
                # One box a run, only with 11 s to spare, and one try a pane per SID_REFRESH: a
                # docker exec costs about 1.5 s and box_sessions waits 10 s at most.
                box_lookups += 1
                seen["looked"] = now
                try:
                    found = resurrect.box_sessions([seen["box"]]).get(pane["id"])
                except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
                    found = None
                if found:
                    seen["sid"] = found[1]
            records[pane["id"]] = dict(seen, kind=kind, target=pane["target"], window=pane.get("window", ""), seen=now)
            continue
        if not old:
            continue
        name = f"pane {pane['id']} ({pane['target']}) session {old.get('sid') or 'unknown'}"
        if kind == "busy":
            # A pane is busy for a moment after its omp dies: the shim's bash finishing, then the
            # `exec bash -i` reading .bashrc (seconds at load 400). Only a pane that stays busy is
            # running something else on purpose.
            busy_since = float(old.setdefault("busy_since", now))
            if now - busy_since < BUSY_FORGET:
                continue
            log(f"{name}: left: the pane has run {seen['comm']}, not omp, for {human(now - busy_since)}; forgotten")
            records.pop(pane["id"], None)
            left += 1
            continue
        reason, resume = restart_decision(old, omps, restarts, options, now, resurrect, holder, records, pane["id"])
        if reason.startswith("wait:"):
            log(f"{name}: {reason}")
            waiting += 1
            continue
        if reason:
            log(f"{name}: {reason}")
            records.pop(pane["id"], None)
            left += 1
            continue
        command = resume_command(resume)
        why = f"agentbox move gave up ({resume['gave_up']})" if resume.get("gave_up") else "its omp is gone and the session did not end normally"
        why += gone_note(resume) + flags_note(resume)
        if dry:
            log(f"{name}: DRY RUN, would resume: {why}: {command}")
            resumed += 1
            continue
        # The pane as it is NOW, not as the listing at the start of this run had it: `agentbox
        # move` hangs a session's omp up and respawns its pane into the box launcher seconds
        # later, and a run that decided in that gap read the old pane pid, found nothing under it,
        # and killed the launcher with its own respawn (01a14b36, pane %7, 2026-11-02T05:38:05Z).
        current = pane_now(pane["id"])
        if current is None or current["pid"] != pane["pid"]:
            log(f"{name}: left: the pane was respawned while this run decided")
            continue
        if pane_processes(current["pid"], resurrect) and not current["dead"]:
            log(f"{name}: left: something started in the pane while this run decided")
            continue
        restarts[resume["sid"]] = now
        state.commit(options)
        ok, error = respawn_pane(pane["id"], resume["cwd"], command)
        if ok:
            heard = ""
            if resume.get("gave_up"):
                place = f"in its pane {pane['id']}"
                heard = report(
                    "resumed", down_resumed_head(resume["sid"], resume["gave_up"], place),
                    down_resumed_text(resume["sid"], resume["gave_up"], place, command),
                )
            log(f"{name}: RESUMED: {why}: {command}" + (f" -> {heard}" if heard else ""))
            old["seen"] = now
            resumed += 1
        else:
            how = f"tmux respawn-pane -k -t {pane['id']} -c {shlex.quote(resume['cwd'])} bash -i -c {shlex.quote(command + '; exec bash -i')}"
            failure = f"tmux respawn-pane -k -t {pane['id']} failed: {error}"
            heard = report("resume-failed", resume_failed_head(resume["sid"], failure), resume_failed_text(resume["sid"], failure, how))
            log(f"{name}: respawn-pane failed: {error} -> {heard}")
            left += 1
    verb = "would resume" if dry else "resumed"
    note = f"panes: {live} running omp, {verb} {resumed}, left {left}, {waiting} held by agentbox move"
    if unread:
        note += f", {unread} not read this run (out of time; the next run starts there)"
    return note


def tell_wall_touch(
    envoy: Envoy, options: argparse.Namespace, state: State, touch: dict, pending_wall: list,
    recent_wall: list, now: float,
) -> bool:
    """Report one wall touch as one message, to the scope's own session; True when it is urgent.

    The box-health owner hears a single touch only when no live session on this box owns the
    scope (send mode), and, in collect mode, when it has one of the two shapes that end in a kill,
    since there its only other view is the collector's hourly fleet digest. Several touches in
    one window reach it as one burst (`tell_wall_burst`). In the 48 h to 2026-11-03 the box-health
    owner got 52 wall messages, 13 of 14 immediate notes were repeated by the 15-minute digest
    within 2 min, and 41 of the 52 had no reaction (dispatch://INFRA-672).
    """
    collect = options.wall_digest_mode == "collect"
    # Before the text exists: a report sent to an id that is not live here reaches whoever holds
    # it elsewhere, not a silent owner.
    verify_touch_session(envoy, touch)
    body = wall_text(touch) + (touch.get("session_note") or "")
    if touch["suppressed"]:
        body += (
            f"\nIt also touched the wall {touch['suppressed']} more time(s) inside the"
            f" last {human(options.wall_cooldown)}, which this one report covers."
        )
    if touch.get("quiet"):
        body += (
            f"\nBefore this, it touched the wall {touch['quiet']} time(s) that were not told: each"
            " with oom and oom_kill 0 and at least"
            f" {100 * options.wall_quiet_reclaimable:.0f}% of the ceiling reclaimable page cache,"
            " a capped job working as designed."
        )
    report = write_report(options, "wall", body, now)
    verdict = wall_verdict(touch)[0]
    entry = {
        name: touch[name]
        for name in (
            "at", "scope", "session", "session_claim", "worker", "peak", "limit", "events_delta",
            "top", "task_count", "rss_sum", "stats", "current", "events_oom_kill",
        )
    }
    if collect:
        pending_wall.append(entry)
        del pending_wall[: max(0, len(pending_wall) - options.wall_pending_max)]
    else:
        row = {name: entry[name] for name in ("at", "scope", "session", "worker", "peak", "limit", "stats", "events_oom_kill")}
        recent_wall.append(dict(row, urgent=verdict == "urgent", report=report, role_told=False))
    state.commit(options)
    text = short_message(wall_head(touch), report)
    owner = touch.get("lane_of") or touch["session"]
    if owner:
        heard = owner if options.dry_run or envoy.send(owner, text) else ""
    elif collect:
        heard = "the collector's fleet digest"
    else:
        heard = "(dry run)" if options.dry_run else tell_box_health(envoy, text)
        recent_wall[-1]["role_told"] = bool(heard)
    who = (
        f"session {owner}" + (f" (claim {touch['session_claim']} is not live here)" if touch.get("session_claim") else "")
        if owner
        else "no live session on this box could be matched"
    )
    log(
        f"{touch['key']}: WALL ({gib(touch['peak'])} peak of {gib(touch['limit'])}, memory.events max"
        f" +{touch['events_delta']}, {verdict}; {who}) -> {heard or 'nobody: every route refused it'}"
    )
    if options.print_alert:
        print(text)
    reason = urgent_reason(touch, options) if collect else ""
    if reason:
        note = short_message(urgent_text(touch, reason), report)
        heard = "(dry run)" if options.dry_run else tell_box_health(envoy, note)
        log(f"{touch['key']}: URGENT ({reason}) -> {heard or 'nobody: every route refused it'}")
        if options.print_alert:
            print(note)
    return verdict == "urgent"


def tell_wall_burst(envoy: Envoy, options: argparse.Namespace, state: State, recent_wall: list, now: float) -> bool:
    """Tell the box-health owner once when --wall-burst touches land on this box inside one
    --wall-burst-window, and again only when that count doubles.

    A burst is the box-level fact one touch is not: one cause -- a build or gate shared across
    lanes, or the box itself short of memory -- behind several scopes. The episode ends when the
    window holds no touch, so a count that dips under the level and comes back is not news. A
    burst made only of touches the owner already heard one by one (scopes with no owning
    session) is not sent again as a digest of themselves.
    """
    window = options.wall_burst_window
    recent_wall[:] = [touch for touch in recent_wall if now - float(touch.get("at") or 0) < window]
    if not recent_wall:
        state.data.pop("wall_burst", None)
        return False
    told = int((state.data.get("wall_burst") or {}).get("count") or 0)
    if len(recent_wall) < max(options.wall_burst, 2 * told) or all(touch.get("role_told") for touch in recent_wall):
        return False
    report = write_report(options, "wall-burst", wall_burst_report(recent_wall, window, options.wall_burst), now)
    text = short_message(wall_burst_head(recent_wall, window), report)
    state.data["wall_burst"] = {"at": now, "count": len(recent_wall)}
    for touch in recent_wall:
        touch["role_told"] = True
    state.commit(options)
    heard = "(dry run)" if options.dry_run else tell_box_health(envoy, text)
    log(f"wall burst: {len(recent_wall)} touch(es) in {human(window)} -> {heard or 'nobody: every route refused it'}")
    if options.print_alert:
        print(text)
    return True


def run_once(options: argparse.Namespace) -> int:
    started = time.time()
    run_clock = time.monotonic()
    envoy = Envoy(timeout=options.http_timeout)
    state = State(options.state)
    if not state.locked:
        log(f"another run holds {options.state}; skipping this period")
        return 0
    scopes_state: dict = state.data.setdefault("scopes", {})
    # Crossing memory, keyed by scope. A scope belongs to one session start (`systemd-run
    # --scope` never reuses a name), and the omp pid and its start time are kept with it so a
    # different process in the same scope starts over.
    incidents: dict = state.data.setdefault("incidents", {})
    # How many separate crossings each SESSION has had. The alert says the number: one session
    # that keeps coming back is a lane problem, not a spike.
    crossings: dict = state.data.setdefault("crossings", {})
    # Whether this state file has no WALL memory at all. On such a run -- the first after this
    # trigger was added, or the first after the state file was lost -- every scope's counters
    # are SEEDED and nothing is reported: a counter already above zero says a touch happened at
    # an unknown time in the past, often before this trigger existed, and reporting the whole
    # backlog at once (31 scopes on ossv-agent-open on 2026-10-30) would bury the owners in
    # history. The crossing state is no part of this test: a state file written by the version
    # before this trigger has every scope in `scopes` and no wall memory at all, and must still
    # seed. A scope that appears while the file already knows others is genuinely new, so its
    # baseline is zero and its first real touch IS reported.
    cold_start = not state.data.get("walls")
    # The wall memory, keyed by scope name and NOT reset with the pid: `systemd-run --scope`
    # never reuses a name, so a key lives exactly as long as the scope does.
    walls: dict = state.data.setdefault("walls", {})
    # collect: the touches waiting for the fleet collector (--wall-pending-json). send: the
    # touches of the last --wall-burst-window, which decide whether the box-health owner hears a
    # burst. Nothing waits for a digest in send mode: each touch was told when it was seen.
    collect = options.wall_digest_mode == "collect"
    pending_wall: list = state.data.setdefault("wall_pending", []) if collect else []
    recent_wall: list = [] if collect else state.data.setdefault("wall_recent", [])
    if not collect:
        # Left by the version with a 15-minute digest: every one of them reached its owner.
        state.data.pop("wall_pending", None)
        state.data.pop("wall_digest_at", None)

    # What each omp process is measured against. Two shapes exist on this box:
    #
    #  * a CAPPED scope of its own (`shims/omp` runs each session under `systemd-run --user
    #    --scope -p MemoryMax=$OMP_MEMORY_MAX`): the scope is the session's whole budget, its
    #    tool subprocesses included, and it is exactly what the memcg OOM killer measures.
    #  * NO scope of its own: the session runs in the login session's scope
    #    (`session-c45.scope` here, 744 processes and 51 GiB of other people's work), which no
    #    cgroup limits. Charging that to one session would alert on every session in the login,
    #    so an uncapped session is measured on its OWN process RSS instead, against the
    #    machine's RAM -- the ceiling that kills it is then the global OOM killer.
    targets = []
    by_scope: dict[str, int] = {}
    omps = omp_processes()
    for pid, cgroup in omps:
        limit, limit_from, limited = effective_limit(cgroup)
        if limit <= 0:
            continue
        scope = cgroup.rsplit("/", 1)[-1]
        if limited:
            # One capped scope is one session: its several omp processes are that session's own
            # subagent lanes, and they share the one budget. The largest is the session itself.
            if scope in by_scope and rss_of(by_scope[scope]) >= rss_of(pid):
                continue
            by_scope[scope] = pid
            usage, current, fields = scope_pressure(cgroup)
            events = memory_events(cgroup)
            # memory.peak is the high-water mark of memory.current. It exists from Linux 5.19;
            # where it does not, memory.current is the best this run has and the peak test is
            # simply weaker, while the memory.events test is unaffected.
            peak = read_int(CGROUP_ROOT / cgroup.strip("/") / "memory.peak")
            targets.append(
                dict(
                    key=scope, scope=scope, cgroup=cgroup, pid=pid, limit=limit, limit_from=limit_from,
                    limited=True, usage=usage, current=current,
                    events=events, peak_bytes=peak if peak is not None else current,
                    swap_max=swap_setting(cgroup), stats=fields,
                    measured="the scope's unreclaimable memory (anon+shmem+kernel+sock), which is what"
                    " the kernel's memcg OOM killer compares with the ceiling",
                )
            )
        else:
            usage = rss_of(pid)
            targets.append(
                dict(
                    key=f"{scope}:pid{pid}", scope=scope, cgroup=cgroup, pid=pid, limit=limit,
                    limit_from=limit_from, limited=False, usage=usage, current=usage,
                    measured=(
                        "this omp process's own RSS. It has NO memory ceiling of its own: it runs"
                        f" in the shared login scope {scope}, so nothing caps it and nothing but the"
                        " machine's RAM stops it"
                        if limit >= mem_total()
                        else "this omp process's own RSS. It has no scope of its own; its ceiling is"
                        f" the box it runs in ({limit_from})"
                    ),
                )
            )

    # Neither pass may fail the watch: an error is logged and the run goes on.
    bias_note = "oom bias: off"
    if options.oom_bias:
        try:
            counts = oom_bias_pass(omps, options.oom_bias_adj, options.dry_run, run_clock + BIAS_DEADLINE)
            bias_note = oom_bias_note(counts, options.oom_bias_adj, options.dry_run)
        except Exception as error:  # noqa: BLE001 -- the alerting below must still run
            bias_note = f"oom bias failed: {type(error).__name__}: {error}"

    alerts = clears = watched = walls_hit = urgent = 0
    for target in sorted(targets, key=lambda t: t["key"]):
        key, scope, cgroup, pid = target["key"], target["scope"], target["cgroup"], target["pid"]
        limit, limit_from, usage, current = target["limit"], target["limit_from"], target["usage"], target["current"]
        # The wall test first, and independent of everything below it: a scope AT memory.max is
        # reported whether or not its unreclaimable memory ever sits above the warn level, and
        # whether or not a crossing is open for it.
        if target["limited"]:
            touch = wall_touch(target, walls, options, started, cold_start)
            if touch:
                walls_hit += 1
                urgent += tell_wall_touch(envoy, options, state, touch, pending_wall, recent_wall, started)
        warn_at = limit * options.fraction
        # The flat level is for a session that NOTHING caps but the machine's RAM. A session in an
        # agent box has no scope of its own either, but its box has a ceiling - the container's
        # memory.max, which effective_limit found up the chain - and since the box default follows
        # the machine (109 GiB on sami-devbox, dotfiles 1d13c4f8) the flat level warned boxed
        # sessions at 16.8 GiB of 109 (XV6 at 19 GiB, 01:56Z 2026-11-04). A ceiling under the
        # machine's RAM is a real one, and the fraction of it stands.
        if not target["limited"] and limit >= mem_total():
            # Nothing caps this session, so "a fraction of the ceiling" would be a number no
            # session on a 251 GiB box ever reaches alone. The level that matters is the one a
            # capped session is warned at: a single uncapped session past it is as dangerous to
            # the box as a capped one is to itself.
            warn_at = min(warn_at, options.unlimited_warn * GIB)
        # Hysteresis: a crossing clears well under the level that opened it, so one session
        # sitting on the threshold cannot send an alert and a clear every period.
        clear_at = warn_at * (options.clear_fraction / options.fraction)
        record = scopes_state.setdefault(key, {})
        if record.get("pid") != pid or record.get("pid_start") != pid_start(pid):
            record.clear()
            record["pid"] = pid
            record["pid_start"] = pid_start(pid)
        samples: list = record.setdefault("samples", [])
        # Samples are kept only once a target is close to its threshold, which keeps the state
        # file small and still gives a measured rise rate well before the alert.
        if usage >= warn_at * (options.sample_fraction / options.fraction):
            samples.append([started, usage])
            del samples[: max(0, len(samples) - options.samples)]
            watched += 1
        elif samples:
            samples.clear()

        incident = incidents.get(key)
        if incident and incident.get("alerted_at") and target["limited"]:
            # Before any clear: an OOM kill can be what ended the session's omp.
            oom_copy(envoy, options, state, key, incident, int(target["events"].get("oom_kill", 0)), started)
        if usage < clear_at or (incident and incident.get("pid") != pid):
            if incident:
                cause = (
                    f"it fell to {gib(usage)}, under the {gib(clear_at)} clear level"
                    if incident.get("pid") == pid
                    else f"the omp process changed: pid {incident.get('pid')} is gone, pid {pid} runs there now"
                )
                if send_clear(envoy, options, key, incident, cause, started):
                    clears += 1
                incidents.pop(key, None)
                state.commit(options)
            continue
        if usage < warn_at:
            # Between the clear level and the threshold: an open crossing stays open, and a
            # target with no crossing is simply not one yet.
            if incident:
                log(f"{key}: {gib(usage)} is between clear {gib(clear_at)} and warn {gib(warn_at)}; crossing stays open")
            continue

        incident = incidents.setdefault(key, {})
        incident.setdefault("opened_at", started)
        if target["limited"]:
            incident["cgroup"] = cgroup
            incident.setdefault("oom_kill_base", int(target["events"].get("oom_kill", 0)))
        incident["pid"] = pid
        incident["scope"] = scope
        incident["pressure_cgroup"] = cgroup
        incident["limit"] = limit
        incident["limit_from"] = limit_from
        incident["limited"] = target["limited"]
        incident["measured"] = target["measured"]
        incident["warn_at"] = warn_at
        incident["current"] = current
        incident["swap_max"] = target.get("swap_max") or ""
        incident["usage"] = usage
        incident["peak"] = max(incident.get("peak") or 0, usage)
        incident["omp_rss"] = rss_of(pid)
        incident["task_count"] = len(scope_tasks(cgroup)) if target["limited"] else 1
        rate = rate_per_min(samples)
        if rate is not None:
            incident["rate"] = rate
            incident["rate_span"] = samples[-1][0] - samples[0][0]
        over_for = started - incident["opened_at"]
        if over_for < options.grace:
            log(f"{key}: over {gib(warn_at)} for {int(over_for)} s (< {options.grace} s grace), holding")
            state.commit(options)
            continue

        if not incident.get("alerted_at"):
            session, omp_pid = scope_session(scope_tasks(cgroup), pid) if target["limited"] else (session_of(pid), pid)
            incident["session"] = session
            incident.update(omp_identity(omp_pid))
            route_lane(envoy, incident)
            incident["self_watch"] = self_watch_of(cgroup, pid) if target["limited"] else self_watch_anywhere(pid)
            count = crossings.get(session or key, 0) + 1
            crossings[session or key] = count
            incident["crossing_number"] = count
            # The session alone: it owns the shedding, and 14 of the 20 alerts the box-health
            # owner was copied on in the 48 h to 2026-11-03 cleared within 4 min. That owner hears
            # a crossing only when nobody else can: no session could be named, the scope is
            # still rising --escalate after the alert, or the kernel OOM-kills in it (oom_copy).
            recipient = incident["lane_of"] or session or BOX_HEALTH_ROLE_TOPIC
            body = alert_text(key, incident, started, "first alert")
            if incident["lane_of"]:
                body = lane_note(incident) + "\n" + body
            if count >= 2:
                body += f"\nThis is crossing number {count} for this session since this watch began counting."
            if not session:
                body += (
                    "\nThe session id could not be resolved from /proc, so the session itself could"
                    " not be told: only the box-health owner has this."
                )
            incident["report"] = write_report(options, "near", body, started)
            text = short_message(near_head(key, incident, started, count), incident["report"])
            # On disk before a single message leaves: a resent first alert is worse than a late one.
            incident["alerted_at"] = started
            incident["alert_usage"] = usage
            incident["told"] = [recipient]
            state.commit(options)
            sent = options.dry_run or tell(envoy, recipient, text)
            alerts += 1
            log(
                f"{key}: ALERT ({gib(usage)} of {gib(limit)}) -> "
                + (recipient if sent else f"nobody: {recipient} refused it")
            )
            if options.print_alert:
                print(text)
        elif (
            started - incident["alerted_at"] >= options.escalate
            and not incident.get("escalated_at")
            and usage > (incident.get("alert_usage") or 0)
        ):
            body = alert_text(key, incident, started, f"STILL RISING {human(started - incident['alerted_at'])} after the alert")
            body += (
                f"\nNobody has shed anything since the first alert at {iso(incident['alerted_at'])}:"
                f" it was {gib(incident.get('alert_usage') or 0)} then and is {gib(usage)} now."
                f" The alert the session was sent: {incident.get('report') or '(no report recorded)'}"
            )
            text = short_message(rising_head(key, incident, usage, started), write_report(options, "still-rising", body, started))
            incident["escalated_at"] = started
            state.commit(options)
            # A copy only: the clear still goes to the session alone, never here.
            heard = "(dry run)" if options.dry_run else tell_box_health(envoy, text)
            log(f"{key}: ESCALATED -> {heard or 'nobody: every route refused it'}")
            if options.print_alert:
                print(text)
        else:
            log(f"{key}: over for {human(over_for)}, already alerted at {iso(incident.get('alerted_at'))}")
        state.commit(options)

    # Forget what is no longer running, so the state file cannot grow without bound. A crossing
    # whose target is gone clears: the session ended, by its own restart or by the kernel.
    live_keys = {t["key"] for t in targets}
    for key in list(scopes_state):
        if key not in live_keys:
            scopes_state.pop(key, None)
    for key in list(incidents):
        if key not in live_keys:
            incident = incidents[key]
            if incident.get("alerted_at") and incident.get("limited") and incident.get("cgroup"):
                # The omp is gone and its scope may still be there, holding the counter that
                # says whether the kernel took it.
                events = memory_events(incident["cgroup"])
                if events:
                    oom_copy(envoy, options, state, key, incident, int(events.get("oom_kill", 0)), started)
            if send_clear(envoy, options, key, incident, "the omp process ended: the session is no longer running", started):
                clears += 1
            incidents.pop(key, None)
    for key in list(walls):
        if key not in live_keys:
            walls.pop(key, None)

    # Several scopes at their wall at once reach the box-health owner as one message. In collect
    # mode this box sends none: its touches wait in the state file until the fleet collector
    # drains them with --wall-pending-json and sends ONE message for every lane VM (Sami,
    # 2026-10-31: eight boxes each sending their own every 15 minutes was 32 messages an hour).
    if collect:
        if pending_wall:
            log(f"wall: collect mode, {len(pending_wall)} touch(es) held for the collector")
    else:
        tell_wall_burst(envoy, options, state, recent_wall, started)
    pruned = prune_reports(options, started)

    restart_note = "panes: restarter off"
    if options.restart:
        try:
            restart_note = restart_pass(options, state, omps, started, run_clock + RESTART_DEADLINE, envoy)
        except Exception as error:  # noqa: BLE001 -- the run's own record must still be written
            restart_note = f"panes: restarter failed: {type(error).__name__}: {error}"

    scope_note = "scopes: adoption off"
    if options.adopt_scopes:
        try:
            scope_note = scope_adopt_pass(omps, state, options, started, run_clock + ADOPT_DEADLINE)
        except Exception as error:  # noqa: BLE001 -- the run's own record must still be written
            scope_note = f"scopes: adoption failed: {type(error).__name__}: {error}"

    broker_note = "brokers: scopes off"
    if options.adopt_scopes:
        try:
            broker_note = broker_scope_pass(state, options, run_clock + BROKER_DEADLINE)
        except Exception as error:  # noqa: BLE001 -- the run's own record must still be written
            broker_note = f"brokers: scopes failed: {type(error).__name__}: {error}"

    state.commit(options)
    state.close()
    log(
        f"checked {len(targets)} omp session(s), {watched} close enough to sample, in "
        f"{time.time() - started:.2f}s; {alerts} alert(s), {clears} clear(s),"
        f" {walls_hit} wall touch(es) ({urgent} urgent), "
        + (
            f"{len(pending_wall)} held for the collector"
            if collect
            else f"{len(recent_wall)} in the last {human(options.wall_burst_window)}"
        )
        + (f", {pruned} old report(s) pruned" if pruned else "")
        + f"; {bias_note}; {restart_note}; {scope_note}; {broker_note}"
    )
    return 0


def drain_pending(options: argparse.Namespace) -> int:
    """Print this box's waiting wall touches as JSON and clear them. For the fleet collector.

    Under the same exclusive lock as a run, so a touch is never printed twice and never lost
    between the print and the clear: the state is written before this process prints a byte.
    Each touch is stamped with this box's short name, which is the collector's only way to say
    which box a line came from. It sends nothing and decides nothing.
    """
    state = State(options.state)
    if not state.locked:
        log(f"another run holds {options.state}; nothing drained")
        print(json.dumps({"box": socket.gethostname().split(".")[0], "locked_out": True, "touches": []}))
        return 0
    box = socket.gethostname().split(".")[0]
    pending: list = state.data.setdefault("wall_pending", [])
    touches = [dict(touch, box=touch.get("box") or box) for touch in pending]
    del pending[:]
    state.commit(options)
    state.close()
    print(json.dumps({"box": box, "locked_out": False, "touches": touches}))
    return 0


def run_move_queue() -> None:
    """Start the `agentbox move --to` jobs a box queued for this host.

    A box starts its move's relauncher on the host through the tmux relay, and a tmux client of
    another version cannot talk to that server (the image's 3.4 against this host's 3.8): such a
    box leaves its job in <cache>/omp/agentbox/move-to/queue instead. This runs each one; the
    watch runs every minute, so a queued move waits no longer than that. A failure is logged and
    never stops the pass."""
    queue = MOVE_LOG_DIR / "move-to" / "queue"
    if not queue.is_dir() or not any(queue.glob("*.json")):
        return
    try:
        done = subprocess.run(
            [str(AGENTBOX), "move", "--run-queue"], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60, check=False
        )
    except Exception as error:
        log(f"move queue: agentbox move --run-queue failed: {type(error).__name__}: {error}")
        return
    for line in (done.stdout + done.stderr).splitlines():
        if line.strip():
            log(f"move queue: {line.strip()}")


def main() -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__, formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
    parser.add_argument(
        "--fraction",
        type=float,
        default=0.70,
        help="alert at this fraction of the scope's ceiling (0.70 of 24 GiB = 16.8 GiB, 7.2 GiB of headroom)",
    )
    parser.add_argument("--clear-fraction", type=float, default=0.62, help="clear below this fraction (hysteresis)")
    parser.add_argument("--sample-fraction", type=float, default=0.45, help="keep rise samples above this fraction")
    parser.add_argument("--samples", type=int, default=24, help="samples kept per scope (24 x 30 s = 12 min of rise)")
    parser.add_argument("--unlimited-warn", type=float, default=16.8, help="GiB at which an UNCAPPED scope alerts")
    parser.add_argument("--grace", type=float, default=60.0, help="seconds over the threshold before it alerts")
    parser.add_argument(
        "--escalate", type=float, default=600.0,
        help="seconds after an alert at which a scope still rising is copied to the box-health owner",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--wall-fraction",
        type=float,
        default=0.98,
        help="memory.peak at or above this fraction of the ceiling counts as reaching the wall",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--wall-cooldown", type=float, default=3600.0,
        help="seconds between wall reports for one scope (one an hour: at 15 min, one capped job"
        " that sat on its wall all night was four reports an hour, 2026-11-04)",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--wall-quiet-reclaimable", type=float, default=0.50,
        help="a wall touch with oom and oom_kill 0 and at least this fraction of the ceiling in"
        " reclaimable page cache is a capped job working as designed: counted, never told",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--wall-burst", type=int, default=3,
        help="send mode: this many wall touches on the box inside --wall-burst-window are told to"
        " the box-health owner as one message, and again when the count doubles",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--wall-burst-window", type=float, default=900.0, help="seconds over which --wall-burst counts touches"
    )
    parser.add_argument("--wall-top", type=int, default=3, help="processes by RSS named in a wall report")
    parser.add_argument(
        "--wall-pending-max", type=int, default=400,
        help="collect mode: touches kept waiting for the fleet collector before the oldest are dropped",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--wall-digest-mode",
        choices=("send", "collect"),
        default="send",
        help="send: a touch with no owning session, and a burst, go to the box-health owner from"
        " this box. collect: it holds every touch for a fleet collector to drain with"
        " --wall-pending-json and send as one message",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--wall-urgent-anon",
        type=float,
        default=0.50,
        help="a touch whose anon is at least this fraction of the peak is urgent; in collect mode"
        " it is sent to the box-health owner at once, not held for the collector (an oom_kill always is)",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--wall-pending-json",
        action="store_true",
        help="print this box's waiting touches as JSON and clear them, then exit; for the"
        " fleet collector. It decides nothing and sends nothing",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--state",
        type=Path,
        default=DEFAULT_STATE,
        help="the owned state file; another path is for tests and never sends",
    )
    parser.add_argument("--http-timeout", type=float, default=8.0)
    parser.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true", help="decide and log, send nothing")
    parser.add_argument("--write-state", action="store_true", help="with --dry-run, still write the state file")
    parser.add_argument("--print-alert", action="store_true", help="print the alert text it would send")
    parser.add_argument(
        "--no-oom-bias",
        dest="oom_bias",
        action="store_false",
        help="do not raise the oom_score_adj of the processes in each capped omp scope",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--oom-bias-adj",
        type=int,
        default=500,
        help="the oom_score_adj every process in a capped omp scope but the session's own omp is"
        " raised to (1-1000; +500 adds half the scope's limit to each one's OOM badness)",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--no-restart",
        dest="restart",
        action="store_false",
        help="do not record omp panes nor resume a pane whose omp died",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--restart-dry-run",
        action="store_true",
        help="decide and log which panes would be resumed, resume none (--dry-run implies it)",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--no-adopt-scopes",
        dest="adopt_scopes",
        action="store_false",
        help="do not move an omp session with no run-*.scope of its own, or one in an"
        " OOMPolicy=stop scope, into a new run-c<session>.scope, and do not move omp worker"
        " brokers and their services into omp-broker-*.scope and run-c<owner>-svc.scope",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--restart-cooldown",
        type=float,
        default=3600.0,
        help="seconds before one session may be resumed again",
    )
    options = parser.parse_args()
    if not 1 <= options.oom_bias_adj <= 1000:
        parser.error("--oom-bias-adj must be between 1 and 1000")
    box_health_owner()  # refuse an unusable BOX_HEALTH_OWNER before any work
    # The once-per-crossing record lives in one file. A run on any other state file has its own
    # memory of what was already said, so it may never send: two state files are what sent two
    # "first alert" messages for one incident on 2026-10-27 at 07:53:20Z and 07:55:18Z.
    if options.state != DEFAULT_STATE and not options.dry_run:
        options.dry_run = True
        log(f"--state is not {DEFAULT_STATE}: running as --dry-run, sending nothing")
    if options.wall_pending_json:
        return drain_pending(options)
    if not options.dry_run:
        run_move_queue()
    try:
        return run_once(options)
    except Exception as error:  # a crashing watch must say so in the journal, not vanish
        log(f"run failed: {type(error).__name__}: {error}")
        return 1


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
